Peter da Silva wrote:
> 
> >Yes, it's meaningful on Unix and done awkwardly with a pull rather
> >than a push.  The receiving environment needs to do some flavor of
> >a source/. on a program or script which emits shell directives.
> 
> OK, you can force a UNIX system to emulate some aspects of the VMS way of
> handling symbolic names. It's not general, every application that needs
> to operate that way has to be specifically coded to implement it, but if
> you wantto insist on using a procedural programming model with global
> variables instead of the "pure function" dataflow model UNIX uses you can
> get that kind of behaviour.

Who said it was an attempt to emulate the behavior of another system?
It is one of the few dynamic mechanisms available when communicating
between otherwise uncoordinated systems/environments if the native
name/value scheme is to be used as is.  For example, if you're using
a CDE desktop, you'll find quite a bit of this.  When the problem
doesn't fit a dataflow model, say, instead, a smalltalk protocol, then
only having a dataflow mechanism is an onerous constraint.  But then
that's the real problem:  systems with blinders, programmers with
blinders.  (And kindly refrain from ascribing any advocacy to this;
I'm a universal critic.)

