>
>
>David Froble wrote:
>> 
>...
>> 
>> Some of the most important parts of your windoz systems are not part of
>> MS Windows.  Many device drivers are provided by the manufacturer of the
>> device.  They do this in order to sell their devices to all those windoz
>> users out there.  They do not provide drivers for any other OS, that I
>> know of.
>> 
>> Not sure how this is handled for Linux and the other unicies.
>> 
>> VMS on IA-32 isn't the major issue.  Drivers for devices on IA-32
>> systems running VMS is the biggest issue.  Having such devices actually
>> match their declared specifications is another major issue.  Windoz may
>> not use all options in a specification, and therefore get away with
>> using the device.  VMS will use any and all stated capabilities in the
>> interest of performance.  Many devices that are used on windoz systems
>> will die on a VMS system.
>
>
>I heard a story some time ago that at about NT 4 the whole layered
>access system (like VMS's user-supervisor-exec-kernel) was broken so
>that software for games could directly access hardware devices and hence
>make the games run faster.  

Nope.  What they did was move the GUI into the kernel for more speed for 
"serious" applications like CAD.  As far as I know, you still need to go
through the drivers (or install new ones) to get at hardware.  (There's
not much motivation to make games run fast on NT/2000; gamers are likelier
to run 95/98/ME.)

>If that is someone's idea of security and
>reliability ...

So it's not as bad as any random application being able to take over the 
machine, but it does mean that GUI bugs can crash the system - doesn't
make you feel too happy about NT server running the same code as NT
workstation.

In fairness, the GUI code in 2000 seems noticeably less fragile than the 
NT stuff; Netscape 4.7 never crashes my whole system the way it did
sporadically under NT.
>

-- Alan

