The restoration appears to be correct, in that the /VERIFY pass of
standalone backup says the restore worked.

One curious symptom: according to the documentation, on bootup the new
system is supposed to type out the model number, the system disk, etc.
Instead, it ends up directly at the date/time dialog (on my machine)
and crashes (on Kevin Handy's).  Kevin has traced this to a timing
loop which is apparently trying to calculate how fast the system is by
running a tight loop of SOBGEQ R0,here, and then seeing how far one of
the interval timers has advanced.  The timer simulation is not (at the
moment) good enough for this to work like a real VAX; and if it did
work, the results would be dependent on the speed of the host system.

Questions:

1. Does a real system print the system information banner before the
date/time request?
2. Is the timing loop part of the process of determining the model
(all CVAX's have a SID of 10, but can vary in clock frequency)?  If
not, what is it for?

Thanks,

/Bob Supnik


