What on earth does X have to do with UNIX scripting? X is a portable network
window system, which was implemented first on both VMS and UNIX, and I run
my X server on NT as often as UNIX these days. It has its own mechanisms
for passing information between program, using the X server as a mediator,
that are entirely unrelated to the UNIX shell. It has its own authentication
and privilege model, entirely separate from UNIX, VMS, or any other OS one
is runing a client or server on.

However I am not surprised that CDE is trying to do something awful to try
and throw data around behind the server's back instead of using the normal
X mechanisms. I don't have anything against OS/2, but trying to emulate OS/2
in X or UNIX is just as futile as trying to make UNIX behave like VMS or X.

You're just providing more support to my argument that you should learn the
native tools and use them the way they work best rather than trying to
shoehorn everything into a single model.

