On Mon, 31 Dec 2001, John McLean wrote:

>
>
> I heard a story some time ago that at about NT 4 the whole layered
> access system (like VMS's user-supervisor-exec-kernel) was broken so
> that software for games could directly access hardware devices and hence
> make the games run faster.  If that is someone's idea of security and
> reliability ....
>
> Can anyone shed any more light on this ?

I think you were told wrong.  It was precisely at NT 4.0 that many
previoulsy supported devices became paper-weights for precisely the
opposite reason.  The addition of the HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer)
made directly twiddling the hardware un-doable.  Things that relied on
doing this (serious things, not games) like anything using the parallel
port for anything other than a printer (like Iomega Tape Drives and
Connectix Eyeball Cameras) all lost their support at that point.

