:> that, but its scripting languages are light years beyond DCL.

: In what way?

Just its ability to do functions which return values is
worth a lot.  In DCL the next best thing we have is CALL
but then you have to set global variable to communicate
return values back to the caller.

Toss in the ability to include other .ksh code into the
current .ksh program as code and you have a much more power-
ful scripting language.

The ability to trap all signals (except for -9) is another
feature that is very useful.  set control=(y) and on control
frequently don't work as expected in .COM programs - the ^Y
may terminate the command running, not the DCL .COM.



: How do I get a shell to check/verify command line options and values (if
