In article <3C2E506A.152D03E0@fsi.net>, "David J. Dachtera" <djesys.nospam@fsi.net> writes:
quoted Peter da Silva quoting him:

>> >How do I get the shell to perform simple numeric calculations? ...string
>> >manipulation (concatenation, reduction ala DCL)? ...conversion from
>> >binary datatypes to string datatypes (ala F$CVxI())?
>> 
>> expr, awk, sed, join, ...
>
>These are external programs, not the shell.

Yeah, but what's the virtue of being shell-only, when there are multiple
shells and you can't guarantee which one you'll be running under.  (In 
VAX VMS, anyway, you can be pretty sure you'll always have DCL and you'll
always have MACRO, so those skills will be portable to every customer
machine.l  You aren't guaranteed to have Korn shell, Bourne shell, 
Bourne-again shell, C shell - any particular shell - on a customer system,
so what's the point?  You'll probably have those external programs, and 
if you don't, you can probably download and install them.)

And why do you care whether things are in the shell or are commonly-used
system programs?

>
>> >...return info. re: the crontab or "atd" (ala F$GETQUI())?
>> 
>> crontab, atq?
>
>"crontab" and "atq" are external programs, not the shell.

Right, but $ SHOW QUEUE is an external program, and so is $ SUBMIT.
So why are those better?

If the virtue is that it's more easily scriptable to have lexical functions,
okay, but in pretty much any Unix shell it's easily scriptable to open a pipe
to some external program to get the data for you and process it in your 
shell script.

So, even as a big VMS fan, I'm not sure I see why this line of argument
justifies a claim of DCL superiority.  (I'd go along the lines of "guessable
commands and regular interfaces" to make that claim.)

-- Alan

