Then, what's the point?

> >> >How do I get a shell to check/verify command line options and values (if
> >> >any) before it invokes the program?
> 
> >> This doesn't make sense on UNIX,
> 
> >Why not? (I suspect the answer may be "for legacy reasons".)
> 
> Because UNIX has a completely different command line model than VMS. A UNIX
> command line is an array of strings, not a structured language construct.

Exactly my point. Other than placing itself between the kernel and the
programmer, teh shell does little or nothing you can't do yourself
(without the shell).

> [snip]
> The UNIX scripting model is a dataflow engine, using the shell as glue to
> bind together small simple applications. You can not talk about shell
> scripting and exclude external programs. You might as well talk about
> driving a car without any roads or traffic regulations.

...but the original poster was comparing DCL to the scripting languages,
not the external utilities they may invoke. Important distinction.
 
> [snip]
> >> , whether you're using a shell or one of
> >> the DCL implementations on UNIX. You might as well ask how you chroot
> >> in VMS.
> 
> >Simple:
> >$ DEFINE/EXEC/SYSTEM SYS$SYSTEM new_path_specification
> 
> What happens if you access DUA0: directly at this point?

Yes. It's still MOUNTed and available. What's your point?

Remember also that the concept of a filesystem "root" does not translate
directly to VMS, which deals with disk volumes rather than the whole of
storage appearing as a single resource beginning at a specific point
("/").

You can even create your own "roots", as in "rooted logical names".

> >> >How do I get the shell to open/process an indexed file (i.e., do C-ISAM
> >> >the way DCL does RMS)?
> 
> >> You call a utility program to do it.
> 
> >Then, the external program is doing it, not the shell.
> 
> Again, you can not make that distinction.

You not only can, you *MUST*!

Try this: find a way to "break" the path to your utility programs
(clobber the PATH, rename /bin to /binbin, or whatever). Now can you "ls
*"? (No.) Can you "echo *"? (Yes.) So, yes, the concept of commands
which are "internal" to the shell vs. those which are not is vitally
important.

Same is true in VMS. If you have CMEXEC privilege, reDEFINE/EXEC
SYS$SYSTEM to SYS$LOGIN: (/JOB or /PROCESS should not matter). Now, can
you SET PROCESS/PRIORITY=4? (No.) Can you SET DEFAULT SYS$DISK:[]?
(Yes.) That's because SET PROCESS invokes the SET.EXE program, which by
default is sought in the SYS$SYSTEM path. SET DEFAULT invokes
"cliroutine setdefalt" (i.e., it's an "internal" command).

> You seem to have a fundamental misunderstanding of the UNIX shell,

Actually, no. I understand it just fine, thank you, as illustrated
above.

