Peter da Silva wrote:
> Because UNIX has a completely different command line model than VMS. A UNIX
> command line is an array of strings, not a structured language construct.

Some "VMS" commands are also arrays of strings. Any foreign command can just
get the strings with the arc/argv mechanism.  And inside some utilities such
as TCPIP, many commands have Unix syntax.


The more wordy arguments of standard VMS commands, as well as their initial
validation prior to image invopcation is an advantage to a serious system
where you don't want to do something very nasty just because you type a letter
in the wrong case in some long command line.


