Nothing really difficult about VMS once you've spent a day or so getting to
grips with the basics.

Pricing is a whole other ball of wax... You can get it for free for private
use, but first you'd need to get hold of Alpha or VAX hardware to run it
on. (Scouring the online auctions does the job for that). For business use
they claim the total cost of ownership is better than many 'nix solutions.
This is because, amongst other things, once the system is running you don't
need to do much maintenance.

The biggest problem with VMS is non-technical. It's the utter lack of
marketing. DEC were dreadful for it, and despite hopes to the contrary
Compaq haven't been any better. Events like the annoncement of the Alpha
EOL have been a big setback to customer confidence and as far as the
denziens of comp.os.vms can ascertain, Compaq only cares about keeping big
customers like the DoD and NSA.

If you want to know how secure it is then have a look at Opcom's report on
how VMS fared at DefCon 9...

http://www.vmsone.com/~opcom/defcon9.htm

Reliability is another big plus for VMS, the Irish National Railway
apparently hold the record with a machine up for 15 years!


