...

> The death spiral works something like this:
>
> Sales drop (possibly due to nothing more than some random perturbation,
> or perhaps a general slowdown in the economy).
>
> The platform's owner responds by cutting costs (e.g. development and
> marketing) in an attempt to maintain profitability.  (Note: this is the
> point at which the spiral may most reasonably be broken.)
>
> This leads to the (warranted) perception in the marketplace that the
> platform is not getting enough attention and (correctly or incorrectly)
> calls its long term viability into question.
>
> As a result, customers and potential customers think harder and/or longer
> about making long term commitments.
>
> Sales drop.
>
> Repeat until done.

That's a description of the spiral when the product's vendor acts at least
semi-rationally and customers do likewise.

In VMS's case, however, the spiral *started* with owner neglect (and active
deprecation of the platform in favor of NT and/or Unix), and the consequent
customer reaction was *profoundly* rational.

- bill





