Everyone's got war stories.

I managed to fix a UNIX system where everything in /bin had been deleted and
nobody was logged in, except one FTP session (the one that had deleted
everything in /bin, on an old ftpd that allowed you to ftp as root). The
hardest part was geting a running shell... I eventually wrote over a program
run from inetd with the shell, and it kept the execute bit, then telnet to
that port and use a similarly FTPed chmod ...

I had another system that a broken vendor install script managed to format the
system disk, all I had was whatever blocks happened to be in RAM or on
a swap partition. Some programs would run, but break if I hit code that
hadn't been executed lately. Managed to save off the configuration files
so we could get everything back working after reinstalling.

It's amazing what you can do in extremis, but it's only vaguely relevant to
whether "cat" is built in to the shell or not.

(ps, if you do lose cat, here's a workaround:

  cat () {
    while read line; do echo "$line"; done
  }
)

