>> >> >How do I get a shell to check/verify command line options and values (if
>> >> >any) before it invokes the program?

>> >> This doesn't make sense on UNIX,

>> >Why not? (I suspect the answer may be "for legacy reasons".)

>> Because UNIX has a completely different command line model than VMS. A UNIX
>> command line is an array of strings, not a structured language construct.

>Exactly my point. Other than placing itself between the kernel and the
>programmer, teh shell does little or nothing you can't do yourself
>(without the shell).

Since that's exactly what it's supposed to do, and very little more, what
*is* your point? DCL is a procedural programming language (albeit one with
fairly primitive control structures) that sometimes runs external programs
to perform commands. The UNIX shell is a nearly functional programming
language that primarily controls the dataflow between external programs.

This model turns out to be extraordinarily powerful, and allows you to build
quite sophisticated programs out of simple components. It's nothing like DCL,
and looking at features of DCL and trying to find them in the shell, or to
criticise the shell for lacking them, is like complaining that your new
minivan doesn't come equipped with a movie theatre and grocery store.

>...but the original poster was comparing DCL to the scripting languages,
>not the external utilities they may invoke. Important distinction.

The original poster was talking about the ease of programming using the
UNIX shell compared to using DCL. Since you can't do anything in the shell
without external programs, it should be obvious to the most naive observer,
at least at this late date in the discussion, that this distinction is
purely an invention of yours and not something intended by the original
poster.

>> >> the DCL implementations on UNIX. You might as well ask how you chroot
>> >> in VMS.

>> >Simple:
