>> What happens if you access DUA0: directly at this point?

>Yes. It's still MOUNTed and available. What's your point?

Then you haven't performed the equivalent of a chroot.

Chroot completely replaces the user-level view of the entire file and
device namespace with the newly constructed subset. If I haven't mounted
da0s2 in that namespace, it's not visible.

>> >> >How do I get the shell to open/process an indexed file (i.e., do C-ISAM
>> >> >the way DCL does RMS)?

>> >> You call a utility program to do it.

>> >Then, the external program is doing it, not the shell.

>> Again, you can not make that distinction.

>You not only can, you *MUST*!

Must I? Why?

>Try this: find a way to "break" the path to your utility programs
>(clobber the PATH, rename /bin to /binbin, or whatever). Now can you "ls
>*"? (No.) Can you "echo *"? (Yes.) So, yes, the concept of commands
>which are "internal" to the shell vs. those which are not is vitally
>important.

But why stop there. Let's unmount all the devices, format all the file
systems, and overwrite all the open shared libraries in memory! Hey, now
you can't do anything!

I completely fail to see why the behaviour of a system that you've
deliberately broken has any bearing on the utility of the shell as a
scripting environment on a working system.

