Having done the VAX/VMS migration to Unix about 21 years ago...
and then moved to training (including training VMS folks to make the
move)...

Here's a quick note written without this old geek's bifocals so
there may be some spelling errors...

If you're coming from MS-DOS I found it was much easier to teach the
string together small utlities using pipe method of doing things.
Shell expansion of wildcards and treatment  of special charsacters
was one point which was difficult.

If you're coming from VAX/VMS (in my pre-OpenVMS days), the idea of
multiple programs executing on multiple processors concurrently wasn't
difficult and permissions were similar concepts along with programs
running with privileges (SGID, SUID).  

Difficulties were in wildcard handling (programs doing it in VAX/VMS 
via a standard set of functions) (Unix using shell expansion and 
the fact that too many file names break Unix utilities -- cp * junk/ 
with a large directory blows stuff up... the use of xargs was a shocker
since it's not easily apparent why it would be necessary.)


