>Which doesn't work in AT&T/USL systems before SVR3 iirc.

Yes, on older UNIX systems the shell recognised that a file was a script
executed it directly. The shell had code vaguely like this:

	pid = fork();
	if(pid==0) {
		...bookkeeping code here...
		for(each directory in $PATH) {
			if (isexecutable(directory/program)) {
				execute(directory/program, program, args...);
				/* Couldn't execute, assume it's a script */
				execute("/bin/sh", "sh", directory/program, args...);
				bailout("can't exec myself! AUGH");
			}
		}
		bailout("command not found");
	}

When there was only one shell this was not an issue. When Berkeley developed
the Pascal and C shells they came up with this idea of using a comment
character to decide whether to call the V6 shell or the C shell, since
the V6 shell only used ":" for comments (actually, these were labels) and
the C shell used "#".

Later, they came up with the idea of adding the magic number 020443 (#! in
ASCII) to the kernel's list of executable file formats... this would of
course read the command interpreter from the first line of the file. This
didn't show up in AT&T UNIX until they did their big "leeching session"
to add BSD interfaces to either SVR3.0 or SVR3.2.

>At least my old SysIII and SVR2 based systems used : at the top of 
>/bin/sh  and # for csh and didn't support #!shellname.

