Let's face the facts folks as I've heard way too much going around
about this topic in not only this thread, but many others.  There's
too much doubt, so let's just get rid of it all.

Let's look at this from a "macro" level, shall we?  VMS will be around
for awhile, that's for certain.  Let me be definitive about the word
"awhile" before I go into details, awhile means at least until *you*
retire.  And of course, "awhile" means different things if you're like
me, a 23 year old programmer for VMS, in which I then can't speculate
for, but I'm targeting the major audience, probably those 30+ people. 
The main point is that VMS makes money, it makes a good enough amount
of money for any small-mid-large cap firm.  No one is going to just
dump it overnight.  Nobody is going to throw it away along with all
its source code and customers into some black hole/bottomless pit. 
VMS is not going to the farthest depths of Hell, never to return.  It
will be here, sitting still, running its tasks 24/7, 30 years out of
30, you better bet your bottom dollar on that one, uncle sam does.

I can't be more confident about that matter.  

Now the other issues.  I never really defined what "be around" means. 
That seems a lot more ambiguous.  And that probably all depends on how
this market performs, if indeed the market continues to slump, "be
around" may very well be for VMS to be around in its current form with
little or no improvements.  Let's face it, there are sexier os-es out
there that claim to do the same, and Compaq/Digital has already
damaged the image of VMS enough.  Linux, UNIX, and even the IBM
high-end OS-es have been coming around for ages.  It would be hard to
catch up to those systems in terms of user favortism, even if VMS were
open-sourced ( as I heard someone suggest earlier in this damned
thread, but that would be a tough thing for money grubbing companies
like hp and compaq to go for, only for companies that are rich like
ibm, and what sun wishes it was ).

If the economy were to turn around say in the new year, effective
immediately, right before the merger voting, I'm sure as hell there
would be a New HP OpenVMS or whatever the hell they decide to call it.
 There would be some dump of money into VMS, but not much.  It would
appear to me that HP/Compaq would want a more streamlined,
industry-standard type of package.  That means UNIX, what every IT
manager has heard of, Linux, the new catch phrase, and win crap.  That
is... unless you customers make enough noise and enough promises of
$$$, that those blood suckers in the top office decide to listen. 
Remember, the New HP will be a merger of bloodsuckers, bigger, badder,
and hairier than ever.  It will be about how to make the most money,
the fastest way possible in order to appease Wall Street in the
shortest amount of time.  And that just may be the IBM way, which
houses like tons of OSes.

Anyway, all you vms customers and sales people should relax--VMS
wusn't raised by no idiot mother, she was raised correctly and can
stand on her own two damn feet!

Whether or not VMS gets sold or not, it seems to me that its future is
quite intact.  The only question will be whether or not it will be New
HP OpenVMS, Compaq OpenVMS, Redhat OpenVMS, IBM OpenVMS, US Army
OpenVMS, Microsoft OpenVMS, Fujitsu OpenVMS, VMS, EDS OpenVMS, Apple
OpenVMS (chuckle, fat chance), Novell OpenVMS, Smurf OpenVMS, Debian
OpenVMS, Suse OpenVMS, or maybe just plain OpenVMS.  Take your pick,
baby!

I say OpenVMS finds some vc money and flies solo from here on out, we
could be a spinoff from HP called OpenVMS Technologies, much like
Agilent Technologies (symbol on NYSE: A ).

  

