nmm1> This is a Frequently Unanswered Question.

I think this is well known: they got cash and less liabilities. It was
the settlement of a patent case brought by DEC against Intel. Intel
would have probably lost it, so they decided to pay off DEC, and DEC got
_a lot_.

DEC got two things from Intel: they offloaded a few things that were
obviously liabilities (the fab, the engineers, ...) that DEC could no
longer afford, and cash ($700m apparently); as a sweetener for Intel,
they got DEC as an IA64 design win:

  http://www.techlawjournal.com/atr/80427intc.htm
  http://news.cnet.com/news/0,10000,0-1005-200-323419,00.html
  http://content.techweb.com/investor/news/1997/10/1027intc.html

Arguably while the stuff sold to Intel was a liability for DEC, Intel
reckoned they could be assets for them.


