Yup... it's in my MOTD along with the folowing:

  "There is no reason for any individual to have a computer in their
   home."

  "There is no real need for sales people.  Customers will be attracted to 
   Good products without assistance."

Both K.O. quotes.  However, VAX/VMS v2.x and later still qualify as
one of the best documented and easiest to learn and use computer systems
I've ever used and administered.

As for the first K.O. probably saw an information utility kind of like
AOL or Compuserve supplying the system access to terminal boxes (kind of
like the Xterminals or smart set-top boxes that appeared later)...since
users have no desire to do backups or software installs or maintenance
on their own hardware.  (A valid viewpoint -- although a bit
overstated.)

As for the second quote... A viewpoint shared my many engineers and
Consumer Reports fans and few CompUsa purchasers or MicroSoft lovers.

As far as the  Unix vs. VMS issue...
Considering I make my living on Unix boxes now... it took years of work
to get me as productive as I was in days with VAX/VMS.
That's pretty high praise for OpenVMS.

And although the DCL command language isn't your favorite (by your
earlier post)... It was basic-like and allowed me to program stuff in it
almost immediately.

Remember the man pages with SysV when released.  Abysmal.  The
error loging barely approached VAX/VMS 2.x quality.  SysIII and V7 were
worse.  Bugs were everywhere and AT&T wasn't fixing them.

I was at Concurrent Computer (nee Interdata/Perkin Elmer) when a major
cron bug was reported to AT&T (1987).  The thing would leak memory until
non-paging SVR2 type boxes like 32xx's would dump cron's core and
silently drop all cron jobs (like backups) in the trash.  

AT&T's answer to their licensee... Tough. Go to hardware with virtual memory. 
We're not fixing it.  Basically, we're in the 3B business now. That's SVR2...
we're only fixing stuff in SVR3 now and we're not supporting non-paging
systems and the bug is not a problem here... and if you want it fixed
fix it yourself and risk breaking compatiblity...  The bug was never
fixed and I wrote a cron-watcher program to loop and do kill -0 on the
cron pid and check the return and to restart cron if the pid ever
disappeared.

Unix was as much of a proprietary one-vendor system as any other at the
time.  It wasn't much better than DEC's OS's or HP's stuff on their
3000's.  Realistically it was the sales guys at AT&T and Sun and HP on
workstations with BSD based stuff that were proposing open solutions
when they knew that without a source license they're not much more open
than DEC's were. 

And TOPS10 and TOPS20 and VAX/VMS were a hell of a lot better
documented. 


Unix got a lot of mileage over low cost distributions to Universities
under the AT&T consent agreement and the BSD enhancements...  If VAX/VMS
was shipped free or at media cost with every VAX sale to a college you'd 
see VMS everywhere today.  


