Mark Crispin wrote:
> 
> On Fri, 21 Dec 2001, John J Francini wrote:
> > Hence, buffer overflows and other nastiness.  This philosophy is by NO
> > MEANS restricted to Unix; it is very much evident in Microsoft-written OSes
> > and applications (like Exchange, IIS, and many others), as well as plenty
> > of other products.
> 
> Including VMS.  The reason why there aren't many buffer overflow attacks
> on VMS is that the target is uncommon and uninteresting to the bad guys.
> It's a form of security through obscurity.
> 
> If VMS had achieved the level of success that UNIX did, and UNIX faded
> into obscurity the way VMS did, then CERT bulletins would be filled with
> the latest VMS buffer overflow attacks, and UNIX users would be snickering
> about VMS insecurity.

Though plausible, it's not the only possible explanation. For example,
that's the same argument invoked by Microsoft and its supporters when
denying that Microsoft's web server is any less secure than a
Unix-based web server. The argument goes: If your software was as
popular as ours, you'd be a bigger target too. However, I saw a recent
report that showed there are many more Apache installations than IIS
installations, yet Apache shows up in CERT advisories far less often
than IIS.

Another possibility is that given all the stock exchanges, state
lotteries, and funds transfers that run on VMS, you'd think that the
places with all the money would be the bigger targets. You'd also
think VMS would be a bigger target given that the vast majority of the
world's CPUs are manufactured under VMS control. Yet it's just not
happening.

In any case, claiming a platform has more security breaches ONLY
because it's more numerous dodges the real issue: sloppy programming
that fails to apply sanity checks.

Even if the greater number of security breaches is fully explained by
the relative frequency of the platform, that still doesn't excuse the
breaches. If you KNOW your box is a much more likely target, then you
KNOW you need to be extra careful.

I don't have much sympathy for other arguments that C somehow causes
these problems. A skilled programming team knows what to watch for,
and deals with it. There's not a programming language in existence
that handles all possible error checking, without any thought or
effort required of the programmer.

--
