Barb,
you are correct. in the 70s/80s The dec concept of SMP was rather 
immature. We had a few variations on the hardware side that would allow 
various means of multiprocessing.  If you go back to the transputer 
stuff, an experiment that was at least interesting in its architecture, 
you find cruncher/compute engine, limited i/o and limited memory.  The 
whole push toward SMP was to provide some real boost in computational
capability without knowing what that really meant. We hung array and
accelerated vector processors off various systems to give them some
enhanced crunch power.  Mean while, we tried to design systems
with the right compute/io balance point.  I maybe wrong but  I don't
believe we ever reached a maturity level in the SMP realm on the 10
family, even with the vaxen.  The alpha boxes and the crossbar
technology
employed in the multiproc boxes is rather impressive and does seem to 
display a substantial level of maturity in some respects. However, the
key imho is the compilers and really the OS.  Perhaps one could argue
that the DS series alphas are just a loosely coupled system in the same
box, I could not argue this either way.  But the OS, the bsd variants, 
VMS, DU/Tru, and Linux all seem to support the multi cpu boxes and make
them perform - depending on the problem of course!!
