And I don't see that the size of the machine it's hosted on is really
relevant. It's not computational resources that are saved by working
in C as much as programmer resources. When you're working at the hairy
edge of assembly-level programming, you need a language like C or BLISS
or PL/M rather than either Assembler or something that doesn't model
the hardware very well.

The fact that these languages kept getting reinvented until one of them
(it happened to be C, though Intel kept PL/M on life support for quite a
long time, and BCPL seems to still have a following) became the obvious
winner is enough proof they're needed.

