[Followups set to comp.os.vms]

What was not well known is that a registry setting for x86 binaries on 
FX!32 was set to require them to be completely loaded into memory before 
  they would run, as opposed to demand paging which was the default for 
native images.

What this meant is that if you had any memory limitations on the Alpha 
that was running NT, that the emulated image would page heavily. 
Attempting to start a second image under emulation under these 
conditions would result in virtully all activity on the memory limited 
system comming to a halt.

John McLean <mcleanj#swissonline.delete.ch> writes:
> 
>>How difficult would it be to have unix programs execute in a VMS
>>environment ?  If this was possible, even with considerable effort,
>>then suddenly huge number applications could be run on a RAS system.


There are quite a few different assumptions that the UNIX programming 
API makes on the environment.  Some of these features are not available 
unless you are in a container environement such as the former POSIX product.

To emulate the set of programs that do match the provided OpenVMS
features is a small matter of writing the program loader and
thunking layer.  If the program was designed for a different hardware
architecture that translation needs to be done also.


But consider that almost all the programs that fit this class exist in 
source code available on the wild wild web, and can be with a little 
effort built to run on OpenVMS.  And this allows them to be tweaked to 
run even better.

-John
