On 23 Dec 2001, Paul Repacholi wrote:
> Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> writes:
> > I'll give you a hint: payload.
> Was this the TOPS-20 password cracking bug? The page fault one?

That bug was in an older version of Tenex, not TOPS-20.  It was well
publicized (including CACM as I recall) and used in as a cautionary
example in OS classes.

In UNIX terms, the bug was that password validation was implemented as
  strcmp (password,get_password (user)) ? SUCCESS : FAIL

By providing a pointer to the password one byte before a page boundary,
and setting the next page to be no-access (instead of the normal "create
private if touched"), if you got a page fault (which you could trap for
instead of having the OS SEGV you) you would know that the first character
was correct.  Since only printing ASCII characters were used in passwords,
that meant that you had at most 95 tries before you got the first
character.  Now, slide back the password pointer one byte and repeat to
discover the second byte of the password.  Repeat until you got the entire
password.

The fix, of course, was to swallow the entire user's password first before
doing the string compare.  Later on, passwords were one-way encrypted.

