Eric Smith wrote:
> 
> You're arguing that it's perfectly fine and reasonable to use a hammer
> to saw 2x4s, because a hammer is well designed to do what it does, and it
> does it well.  However, it doesn't saw 2x4s well.  When you want to saw
> 2x4s, you use a saw, not a hammer.
> 
> <snip>

The analogy doesn't apply very well to this particular argument. C is a
compiler, likewise any other compiler out there. They all compile.
The difference between a hammer and a saw can not be used to illustrate
the difference between one compiler and another, at least they both
compile. A hammer hammers in nails, a saw cuts wood. That's like comparing
MACRO and PIP.

If you are going to write code in C, you better know that it does no
checking for you on bounds and you better do it right yourself. That's
it.

If you want a compiler that does all the checking for you, be my
guest and write one.

My entire point is this: "If you write C code, it's your fault if
you don't check bounds"...

period.

Merry Christmas!

