On 21 Dec 2001, Bob Ceculski wrote:
> are you saying you can overflow buffers on vms and create privs you never had
> to begin with?  this isn't windoze you know, you need privs to do anything
> on vms ...

It may surprise you, but NT has privileges just like VMS.  Perhaps you are
too young to know about the relationship between VMS and NT.

I won't clue you in on that point (you'll have to find that out on your
own), but I will clue you in on how security gets broken.  It has nothing
to do with a user task getting corrupted by something like a buffer
overflow.

It has everything to do with a privileged task being subverted to do the
bad guy's bidding.  One of many ways is to cause a buffer overflow in the
privileged task.  Another way is to identify a flaw in the means by which
a privileged task determines the authorization of a user request.  Yet
another is to hide an unauthorized payload inside an authorized request.

I'm only scratching the surface here, but you should be able to get the
gist.

