Since you snipped the part of my original post where I mentioned
that the programmer has a choice between truncating the source
string to fit or handling it as an error when the source is too
long, you are clearly here only to troll and not to help other
people write better programs.

But if the user-provided string is too long, and your program
ignores part of it and processes the rest, I don't see how this
can result in a security problem[*].  The result clearly won't
be what the user wanted, but that's a good thing, since he was
trying to exploit or crash your system.

[*] Unless something farther down the chain remembers the original
length of the source string, but looks at the contents of your
fixed length (and too small) buffer.  If that happens, you are
not using counted strings correctly (taking length of string B
and assuming that's the length of string B), which means you
aren't using counted strings, just paying lip service to them.

