Sorry for the delay.  I had to think long and hard about this.

I speak about an _ideal_ timesharing system since, in reality,
all implementations have to have tradeoffs; it also avoids
reproducing the OS war we're currently having in another thread ;-).

An ideal timesharing system, gives each user the warm feeling that
all computing resources are immediately available for his/her use; 
the ideal timesharing system does this for all users at the same time.

An ideal timesharing system can do asynchronous things synchronously
and synchronous thing asynchronously.

An ideal timesharing system is merely a tool for the user and acts
accordingly.  In other words, the user doesn't have to wait nor
wrestle with the OS to get his real work done.

/BAH

