clustering = in PDP10 land back in the bygone era = was called loosely
coupled
multiprocessing.  I don't recall the origination of the term clustering, 
I don't recall the origination of the terms Loosely coupled multiproc or
symetrical multiprocessing...I don't want to drop a name and use the 
wrong name, my recollection is a collection of folks kicked the ideas
around.

Small point of order, your use of the phrase "these are unix people ... 
they don't understand "real" clustering" is a rather trite troll, quite
beneath what I have come to expect from yoru posts.  This thread 
has contributors and readers who might object to such a stereotype.

I will give you some slack tho, there are a number of NT products now
available that bring new meaning to the term "clustering".  I believe
a couple of those vendors may be basing their use of clustering as a 
term of art on a different application, the famous Fuster Cluck.

VMS folks really did perfect clustering and turn it into a distributed,
high availability tool.  Really does work. Kudos for that. The usual
Sun weenie trolls not withstanding any dec or vms news group discussion,
beowolf and other clustering efforts in the linux and BSD worlds are 
attempting to get that same level of reliability and availablity out of
commodity hardware.  Certainly not a bad effort and worth the results.

Seriously, if we step back and look at dec HW, we have stuff that was
designed to be available.  We made an awesome amount of profit off of 
selling service contracts that were really insurance policies that well
designed systems would provide little need for more than regular PM.

If we look at all the OS developments, we certainly have some industry
leading innovations, we have some me too stuff, and we had some god
awful
stuff.  But the categorization of what falls into what category is not
mine to make = I am biased.  I lived thru the killing off of various
products, cultures and the move towards a borg centric effort - all in
the
name of good business.  Does not change my feelings.  My alternative 
views on what could have been or might have been can not be resolved...
the time is gone.
bob


