> determined by marketing expediencies is deemed more important than spending
> the time to do a correct design and to test every conceivable failure mode.  
> It's evident any time that developers decide to not bother writing error
> checking code because it's "not interesting" and are allowed to get away
> with it because doing so will impact schedules.
>
I came across similar examples in NT a few years ago. Not just buffer overflows
but the opposite when a stray null got into a string, effectively truncating
it (I call it buffer underflow (tm) :-) ). 

I can distinctly recall tracking down an example of file date/time conversions
in NT (they are quite sensibly held on disk as UTC date/times in NTFS). With
my background it was quite a surprise when the example I found did _no error
checking whatsoever_. Needless to say, my rewrite of it did.

Actually the buffer "underflow"/overflow problem has also stung me with COBOL 
programs on VMS where someone from a C background decided not to bother
checking a length parameter, but look for a null instead. Nasty.

I've always been used to passing binary buffers to and from subroutines,
usually by one of the following methods:

o - counted strings
o - a length parameter
o - passed by descriptor as per the VMS calling standard (if on VMS)

And always returned some kind of error code, which I check.

Guess what? I've disliked null terminated strings ever since I came
across them, at that was long before I picked up my first book on C.

Further, when I was supporting literally hundreds of COBOL programs, I quietly
sneaked in the compiler options /CHECK=(PERFORM,BOUNDS). The resulting
error reports made fixing problems in those areas child's play. The processor 
overhead was trivial in comparison to the rest of the workload.

