"Dave Cherkus" <cherkus777@777unimaster.com> wrote in message
news:Xns917F79F8FAADidtoken@199.125.85.9...
> gce <ge@gce.com> wrote in news:3C23EA72.48874137@gce.com:
> > Not so.
> > The reason for the lack of buffer overflows is due to a number of
> > factors. One: VMS habitually has used descriptors to pass strings,
> > which have sizes passed.
>
> Interesting.
>
> Was this true in the PDP11 operating systems?

Certainly on RSX, though RSTS may have had greater affection for ASCIZ
strings.

Where they could be used safely (and efficiently, which was not always the
case), they did have advantages.  Byte-by-byte processing is at least as
efficient when dealing with a short string as the set-up involved in trying
to process it word-by-word, and condition codes make the loop-termination
check close to free (and without requiring the use of a counting register, a
resource which the 11 did not have in great profusion).

- bill







