Sue Skonetski wrote:
> 
> The only thing that is killing the OpenVMS Customer base is people
> complaining and saying that Digital/Compaq/HP are going to kill VMS. Do you
> realize every time someone posts a sky is falling message we get calls and
> emails from our customers which we then have to work with to keep as
> customers, stopping us from doing other work (like porting work)? Every time
> a negative stream starts our competition is glad and they can say "see I
> told you so." No, I do not have my head in the sand, but I would prefer to
> defend VMS against a foe instead of a friend. Lets not loose the war because
> of friendly fire.
> 
> Sue


Sue,

Yesterday I said that probably the main reason why you have to spend so
much time defending VMS is because marketing isn't doing their job
properly.  I've been thinking about that today and it seems to me that
there is really plenty that one can complain about with Compaq's
marketing in general and of VMS in particular.

I have now started to wonder what it is that Compaq's marketing people
actually do to earn their salaries. From what I can gather, Compaq is
spending more than $250 million per year on "marketing" and despite
this, current and potential customers are not being properly informed.

A recent case in point is longtime VMS advocate and user Brian
Schenkenberger's Usenet post  - "You wanna know HOW bad Compaq/digital
information is about VMS?  I was at a bank in NYC for about 10 days in
the beginning of December.  One of the folks in the IS dept. there asked
me if VMS was ever successfully ported to Alpha and, if so, how does it
work?  They're still on VAX doing their funds transfers."

Enough said!

And these are incumbent VMS customers to boot.  The marketing to the
existing customer base for NSK seems far superior to that for VMS. 
Could this be why NSK has been doing reasonably well in the current
climate while VMS has been sliding? Sure, high availability has
increased in importance since the events of September 11, but VMS is a
solution that is eminently qualified to capitalize on this new factor in
the plan-to-purchase decision. Based on recent actions, it seems that
Compaq would rather issue a daily PC-centric press release than make
some bold statements about the progress in matters related to VMS and
enterprise systems.

As you well know, Compaq on a profit margin basis derives eight to ten
times more ROI from Enterprise systems and services than from PCs.  If
ever there was a time to make all efforts to increase sales at the
high-end, then this year has been such a time.  But instead of an
increased visibility of high-end offerings, we've been treated to simply
more of the same - huge emphasis on the loss-making PCs and
near-invisibility of anything else.

A key goal of an effective marketing effort is to support, and
contribute to, a firm's business strategy and to give advice on the best
method for selling the firm's offerings.  In this role I expect that
marketing were largely responsible for dividing the sales field into PC,
Unix, VMS and NSK markets, a structure that appears to make life easier
for sales people but does the entire company a great disservice.  If
there was ever any reasonable justification for this division then that
time has past.  In the current economic climate Compaq should be very
happy to sell any platform to any customer, regardless of their activity
sector. 

Compaq's past history in this area has been to try to pressure customers
into buying systems at a lower level than the customer really wants. 
The irony of this situation is that the customer is pressured out of a
high-profit item (like VMS), not even into a medium profit platform
(like Unix) but into very low margin (or even loss-making) products like
Windows based systems.  Not only does this strategy result in
significantly lower profit margins, it is the antithesis of account
control because others have a greater influence over the product and the
sales operation.

The irony in all this is that about two years ago Compaq declared that
it wanted to be known as more than just a PC company.  What does Compaq
consistently do ?  It emphasizes its PCs and then complains that the
industry, market and analysts are just not getting the picture.  Actions
speak louder than words and I'm sure they all got the picture very
well.  Compaq might as well have completed the picture by putting a huge
sign right inside their Houston headquarters to say "Intel Inside" -
visible of course through the massive picture Windows.

If you want the world to know that PCs are just one part of Compaq then
you need to increase the exposure of all other products and services. 
Some people say that you should not advertise specific platforms but
concentrate more on the apps and solutions; they use IBM with its
non-specific advertising as a case in point.  The difference is that IBM
is already synonymous with enterprise systems while Compaq is synonymous
with low-end PCs.  To break this perception Compaq needs to tell the
world in no uncertain terms that it has far more to offer than only PCs.
(Of course customers buy "solutions," but unless customers are aware of
the quality ingredients--e.g. VMS, NSK, management software, services,
storage, etc.--that make up these solutions, they are far more likely to
purchase from a perceived solutions provider, which again would be IBM.

Finally, let's take a look at the impact of the proposed merger.  In the
firm's last financial statement (SEC form 10-Q), under the heading
"Factors That May Affect Financial Condition and Future Results", Compaq
stated "The announcement of the planned merger could have an adverse
effect on Compaq's revenues in the near-term if customers delay, defer,
or cancel purchases pending resolution of the planned merger with HP." 
Note in particular one admonition because the caveat certainly applies
to VMS; it reads "While Compaq is attempting to mitigate the risk
through customer assurance programs, prospective customers could be
reluctant to purchase Compaq's products if they are uncertain about the
direction of the combined company's product offerings and its
willingness to support and service existing products".

Compaq has clearly stated that it recognizes this as a potential problem
but what action has it taken in this regard?  VMS was very conspicuously
absent from the HP position statement about the merger.  All other
operating systems were mentioned several times but there was no sign of
VMS.  What message do you think this is giving potential purchasers of a
high-end, high-margin product?  For that matter, what message do you
think this might give to existing VMS customers ?  According to various
media reports customer defection has already started, so I guess the
answer is rather obvious.

My advice, for what it is worth, is to demand accountability and results
from senior marketing people before they cause the complete collapse of
Compaq.  Compaq's hard-working employees-and the firm's
stockholders-deserve nothing less.


John McLean


