Dave Cherkus wrote:
> 
> gce <ge@gce.com> wrote in news:3C23EA72.48874137@gce.com:
> > Not so.
> > The reason for the lack of buffer overflows is due to a number of
> > factors. One: VMS habitually has used descriptors to pass strings,
> > which have sizes passed.
> 
> Interesting.
> 
> Was this true in the PDP11 operating systems?

Most "system services" that deal with strings (i.e. filenames)
under RSX-11 and RT-11 are working with fixed-size buffers,
usually with RAD50 encoded strings.

> I ask because the first place I saw the practice of zero-terminated
> strings was when using a PDP11 MACRO assembler, which provided the
> ASCIZ directive (ascii string, zero terminated).

Yes null-terminated strings certainly are common on -11's, even if
the OS didn't use them.

> I always thought that's where UNIX picked up the practice, since
> UNIX was mainly written on a PDP11, and perhaps used MACRO as a
> bootstrap language.

It was very common to do null terminated strings on -11's because
of the ease of using the idiom

10$:	MOV (R1)+,(R2)+
	BNE 10$

to, for example, copy a null-terminated string.  Note how this
looks almost identical to the K&R C example of copying a null-
terminated string!  Despite this similarity I think I've seen
Mr. Ritchie write that the C (B?  BCPL?) postincrement syntax
predated the arrival of their -11 in New Jersey.

Tim.


