>Generally speaking, boosting privs is the way to go.  But not easy to do.

I imagine it's gotten a lot better, when I was using VMS there are a heck
of a lot of apparently innocuous privileges that let you get pretty much any
other privilege on the system. I don't recall the details now, but they weren't
all as obvious as the right to grant privileges. :->

>But show me a model that is as flexible, yet so secure?  No doubt that if
>VMS was as popular as Windows, and 17 year olds could read the source like a
>bible, this is where I suspect people would focus.

>> Properly implemented, UNIX has the
>> potential for being a lot more secure,

>Why do you believe this?

What do you mean "why do I believe this"? Do you want to know why I believe
that I have the experience to make such a claim, or do you want a quickie
tutorial on the UNIX security model, or what? I can try and oblige.

The short answer is, UNIX provides a finely grained security model based
on assigning resources to groups, restricting membership in these groups
to people who are authorised to use these resources, and temporarily granting
rights to operate as group members to trusted programs that run with modified
privileges.

There are significant problems in the implementation of this security model
in most applicatons and systems. The failures fall into two main classes:

	1. Resources that can not be assigned rights based on groups.

	   This includes system calls that are super-user only (such as
	   mount) rather than based on access to device files: if you have
	   r/w access to /dev/fd0c, then you should be able to mount it[1].

	   This also includes resources (like TCP/IP sockets) that are not
	   in the UNIX namespace and should be.

	   This also includes resources (like the frame buffer) that have an
	   inadequate protection model.

	2. Lazy programmers.

	   The line printer daemon should not need to run setuid root.

	   The mail server should not need to run setuid root.

	   The name server should not need to run setuid root.

Properly implemented, with all resources subject to file system access
control and programs granted no more privileges than they need to get the
job done, and with processes running with extra privileges properly
designed and audited (which also means making them small enough TO audit:
things like the X server are far too complex to run with root privileges),
the only need for root access would be to introduce new resources and grant
access (adding users, creating devices, etc), and these are rare events.

[1] This would mean that non-root mounts would have to be limited to
    mounting file systems that don't contain resources or mechanisms
    for granting enhanced access. NOSUID,NOXEC,NODEV, to begin with.

