As far as I can remember, RSTS didn't use ASCIZ internally anywhere.

Certainly not in the monitor interfaces, which were all either fixed
length strings (logical names, device names, send/receive receiver
names, etc.) or were passed by length & address (CCL parsing,
filename parsing, i/o and send/receive buffers, and core common.)
Embedded nulls were not special characters and did not cause any
particular problems.  (They were illegal in file names, but were handled
just like the dozens of other illegal characters -- nothing special.)
Embedded nulls in I/O buffers and send/receive messages were normal
and posed no problems.

BASIC+ didn't use null-terminated strings either.

> Where they could be used safely (and efficiently, which was not always the
> case), they did have advantages.  Byte-by-byte processing is at least as
> efficient when dealing with a short string as the set-up involved in trying
> to process it word-by-word, and condition codes make the loop-termination
> check close to free (and without requiring the use of a counting register, a
> resource which the 11 did not have in great profusion).
