No, you didn't.

What I said is that "UNIX is potentially a lot more secure than VMS". The
word "potentially"

In practice the VMS security design problems (mostly due to the large
array of interconnected privileges that in combination provide rights that
were never intended) are more than made up for by the quality of the
implementation. VMS benefits from all the strengths of the "cathedral"
design philosophy, and those strengths (despite Raymond's comments) are
not negligable.

And yet there have been securitiy problems, simply because the security
model is so complex.

In practice the potential of the UNIX security model is frittered away by
people who have no idea how it's supposed to work, and all the weaknesses
of the bazaar design model... and those weaknesses, particularly in the area
of security, should not be minimized.

Given the way live UNIX systems are built of components designed like this,
the fact that it *can* be secured at all is an homage to its strength. It's
a simple and versatile model and properly applied... using subsystems each
running with minimum privilege and with no trust relationships between them...
it's capable of almost infinite flexibility.

And yet a system that has all the weaknesses of the "cathedral" model tied
to all the weaknesses of the "bazaar" model, and none of the strengths of
either, is widely touted as the way of the future.

I'm talking about Windows here, if anyone's lost track...

