David Skinner wrote:

> I've looked around for the correct way to wire one of those do-it-
> yourself MMJ to DB9 adapters - the ones that come with six flying wired 
> pins that can be snapped into the DB9 end in any combination.


I would recommend looking at the OpenVMS FAQ that can be found from a 
link at http://www.openvms.compaq.com.

Wiring diagrams for all of the popular permutations of the MMJ connectors.

Note that there is both a 9 pin PC-AT layout, and an older Digital 
specific layout.

> Unfortunately I've found various conflicting info, a few admitted errors, 
> and a little confusion over whether any grounding pins need to be 
> connected together,


There are two signal returns on the MMJ cable, and they both must be
connected to the signal common on the adapter.  One signal return is for
outgoing data signals, the other for the incoming data signals.


The MMJ signal is RS-423, which is a differential signal that cancels
noise, when connected between two RS-423 devices.

When connected through an adapter that connects the two signal returns 
together, it become compatable with RS-232, but the length that you can 
run the signal is reduced to RS-232 limits.


The signal return lines and the frame ground pin should never be
connected in the cable.

> and whether I'll need to connect null-modem style or 
> whether the DEC cables are already wired this way.


Most MMJ cables have a built in twist in them.  If the adapter is wired 
normally, then nothing special is needed.

For some reason, some A-B or A-B-D-E serial line MMJ switches requre an
untwisted cable to hook them up.


