Arthur Krewat <krewat@bartek.dontspamme.net> writes:
> Yeah, but come on. Isn't it the programmer's fault that they can't write
> good C code? 

Of course it is.  But the reality is that it's going to get written badly
by those programmers.  So the responsible thing for the rest of us to do is
to give those programmers tools that can protect them (and their victims,
er, end users) from the most obvious stupid errors.  The C language isn't
the right tool.

If you knew that most furniture was going to be made by klutzy people,
you'd give them table saws that have finger guards, right?

> Again, I see nothing wrong with the language except that it doesn't check
> on the assholes. 

As I pointed out before, even experts slip up from time to time.  So tools
that can catch the more obvious screwups are preferrable to brain-damaged
tools that can't.

> Seen Ada, matter of fact had to tell the weenies how to program Ada in 
> an effective way (system admin for a place that did DOD stuff).

Sounds like you're talking about efficiency.  You can write inefficient
code in any language, just like you can write buggy code in any language.
There are several areas in which Ada implementations (and the language)
tend to be criticized for inefficiency.  In actuality, there is nothing
in the language spec that can't be implemented just as efficiently as
C code.  People tend to complain about exceptions and generics, which
actually have little run-time overhead.  They also complain about garbage
collection, which is not actually required by the language.  However,
I would argue that garbage collection is actually another thing that
is well worth its small runtime cost.

The usual criticism of garbage collection turns into complaints that
it isn't suitable for real-time systems, which is a red herring.
Memory management in real-time systems has to be done carefully whether
you have garbage collection or not.  Ada implementations let you
explicitly deallocate your own memory (using the unchecked_deallocation
generic), or you can plan your software to defer collection to
acceptable times.  The former is certainly no worse than C, and the
latter is reasonably easy and less error-prone.

> I like machine language, preferably in octal (for PDP-10's).

You're never going to convince a non-trivial number of programmers
of that being the Right Thing (tm).  And I would argue that while
that might be fine for you, you wouldn't really want to depend on
the unwashed masses programming in that.  The results would be much
worse that what we have with C.

> I never liked a compile/interpreter that pretended to know more than 
> I did.

Are you making the "I don't want the compiler to tell me what I can
and can't do" argument?  That's the usual argument of assembler and
C programmers against strongly typed languages.  The counterargument
is that while the compiler isn't smarter or more knowledgeable than
the programmer, it is certainly better at mundane housekeeping tasks,
and thus good at keeping track of data types and letting you know when
you might have made a boo-boo.

Generally people that run into a lot of problems with strong typing
don't understand their problem or data well enough.  In many programs
there is a legitimate need for a small amount of type casting (between
arbitrary types, e.g.  Ada's unchecked_conversion generic,, not simple
numeric casts as in C), but if you have to do a lot of it your program
isn't well-structured.  I've spend uncounted hours rewriting poorly
structured code supplied by outside vendors, and invariably the results
are cleaner, easier to understand, and have a lot less type casting.


