So Unix is a better number cruncher than VMS. In what way is that a sign
of a more advanced technology?

> Now keep the religious biggotry elsewhere.

Both sides are religiously repeating their mantra. Only that some don't
buy Mark's "*ix is technologically more advanced".

*ix is a heap of utilities built around some very simple concepts
(e.g. everything is a byte stream that can be piped). The simpleness
of these concepts sure has its beauty, but I wouldn't call it advanced
technology.

VMS' technology is built on top of some very careful and far-sighted
designs (e.g. a quadword for timestamps, the (distributed) lock manager).

*ix to me seems more like "okay, we screwed it in that matter - so let's
put another layer atop" (thinking of shadow passwords). Of course *ix'
flexibility is good for such "solutions" - but such kludges must not be
taken as advanced technology.

