In article <9vuom7$htmdu$1@ID-56200.news.dfncis.de>,
Martin Vorlaender <martin.vorlaender@pdv-systeme.de> wrote:
>Yes: "Is UNIX technologically superior to VMS?"
>And the answer is: Never, no way.

There are many areas in which VMS is superior, yes. But there are also
areas in which UNIX is superior. For example, on VMS creating a complete
new process context is an expensive event. UNIX is by no means a
microkernel design, but on the continuum from something like VM/CMS on
on hand, and something like QNX on the other, UNIX is definitely much
further along than VMS.

VMS sees a process context as something to be cherished, with one program
after another passing through it, and new processes to be created only when
needed. UNIX is oriented towards the quick creation and teardown of tasks,
and the use of processes to represent separate sections of a complex task.

So, while VMS has added a (somewhat clumsy) pipe mechanism, and other
UNIX features, we're not talking of VMS now: at the time Marc's writing
of it was painful even to implement "Make" because it wasn't even clear
how one might run a compile and get the result of the operation.

I had a set of Software Tools ports I'd done in Fortran on RSX-11, and
porting them to VMS was really unpleasant.  Some I would have left in RSX
emulation forever, but that support was going away, so for "Make" I had
it actually create a DCL script from the Makefile, and ran that.

The UNIX pipes and filters model, and the Software Tools approach to solving
problems, was a major advance. Moving from TOPS and VM/CMS and CDC mainframes
to UNIX was quite a shock, but once I caught on to the design of the system
I wondered why everything wasn't built that way. VMS may have been one of the
most advanced of the monitor designs, but that technology was already being
supplanted by the time it came out.

