It isn't clear how much of that was Samsung overpromising and how
much it was Compaq not taking up an offer.  And possibly how much
was Samsung attempting to pressure Compaq.

As I read that at the time, it was a statement that Samsung could
deliver, if Compaq wanted to buy, and NOT that it was going to
deliver anyway.  I.e. that it had processes that could be got into
production by that date if the decision was taken to do so.

And, as I read the 1.2 GHz announcement, it was a public statement
of "We have got fed up waiting for Compaq, and have overdelivered
anyway - Capellas, get a move on."

Now, whether either statement was founded in hard facts, I cannot
say.  I agree that we can place much more trust in IBM's claims.

But the fact is that Compaq did not have a chipset or board that
could make use of even a 993 MHz Alpha until 2001 makes me think
that the failure of that preposterous claim is at least as much
down to Compaq as Samsung.  After all, why produce a chip that
is far faster (and hence more expensive) than anyone can use?


