In article <9vvu6g$rnk$1@joe.rice.edu>,
Jerry Leslie <leslie@clio.rice.edu> wrote:
>Why wouldn't the bad guys be interested in VMS systems that run a large
>precentage of the lotteries, some stock exchanges, and some banks ?

1. The guys who do most of the buffer overflow attacks are more interested
in getting a UNIX or Windows box they can use to attack other bad guys IRC
channels.

2. I would *hope* critical systems like that aren't exposed to the public
Internet.

3. Exploits like this are more likely to be covered up, and since they
don't produce publicly visible hacked web pages they may never be noticed
outside the victim's staff.

I have been told of a couple of cases where a critical system was left
exposed, and bad guys managed to rootkit/backorifice it... and completely
ignored the system's potential (and almost certainly had no idea what they
had access to) in favor of using it for pingflood amplification.

I'm not at liberty to name names, for obvious reasons, but the systems were
a good deal more worrisome than mere banks and stock exchanges.

Now this isn't to say that I agree with Marc that VMS is equally susceptible
to buffer overflow attacks. Non-executable stacks do help somewhat (but
as he noted, they don't stop them), and the code is written in a wider
variety of languages, many with stronger type structures than C. One issue
I have with VMS and security is the complexity of the security model makes
privilege boosting attacks more likely. Properly implemented, UNIX has the
potential for being a lot more secure, though a lot of UNIX code runs with
far greater privileges than it really needs to. Oh, and the Berkeley sockets
interface is an amazingly poor fit for UNIX.

-- 
