On 21 Dec 2001, Peter da Silva wrote:
> Once you have cache, though, this doesn't save you anything and has the
> disadvantage of making it very difficult to pipeline accesses to the
> accumulators, and someone else has already mentioned what this does to
> separate I&D caching.

On the hardware implementations of the PDP-10 in which this was an issue,
the implementors simply did not bother worrying about it.  They just shut
pipelining off whenever the PC went down there.

The number of programs which ran code in the ACs was extremely limited,
and such programs took a performance hit in those processors.  But there
were only a few which ran code in the ACs for performanace on the older
processors, and it was easy enough to make a version that didn't do that.
The majority did it for some form of bootstrapping, meaning that the total
performance hit for the entire run of the program was a one time hit of a
few microseconds.

In other words, all that was important was that running in the ACs
*worked*, not that it pipeline or be fast.

Of course, when you have hardware engineers who don't have a clue as to
how people actually program for a particular architecture, you get false
statements such as "you can't make a fast PDP-10 because it can run in the
ACs."

The real difficulty in building a fast PDP-10 is in the I/O instructions
and especially the pager.

