>	Are you attempting to exploit (buffer overflow) an existing program
>	on VMS?  Go from theory to example...

Sigh...  What is it with illiterate weenies of all sorts?

Let me repeat it in slowly.  Making data segment non-executable does not
make buffer overruns less harmful.  What it _does_ provide is a protection
against one class of attacks, namely ones that place code to be executed
directly into the buffer.

However, program that is vulnerable to attacks of that class is vulnerable
to slightly different sort of attacks.  One that is _not_ prevented by
having data segment non-executable.

Search the bugtraq archives.  Find any instance of the (recurring) thread
with "non-executable stack" in subject.  Read it.  Think for a while.
Notice that while details depend on calling conventions, vulnerability
itself is pretty much the same, regardless of the OS.

There are systems (including several Unices, BTW) that have non-executable
stack and data segments.  There are patches that provide that for other
systems.  And there are half-literate advocates that happily claim that it
makes $OS more secure.  Bullshit.  Stack-smashing is stack-smashing and if
your program allows it - it can be tricked into doing what attacker wants.
With priveleges it's run with.

