In article <Pine.NXT.4.50.0112211422100.6928-100000@Tomobiki-Cho.CAC.Washington.EDU>, Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> writes:
:On Fri, 21 Dec 2001, Alan Greig wrote:
:> I don't believe anyone should publicise full details of exploits, 
:> if not already in the public domain, for any operating system.
:
:Regardless of whether or not you or I believe that exploits should be
:publicized, the fact is that they are.  That's the world that we live in
:today.  If VMS security bugs are shielded from publication, that renders
:into nonsense all claims of VMS being secure.  It's just security through
:obscurity.

  There have been various previous discussions on this exact topic, and 
  your opinion has been quite regularly represented.  Your points are 
  quite valid.  You are entirely correct that some number of so-called 
  black hats will know about security bug(s) at the speed of email.  
  (Some will quietly sit on the "best" holes, but I digress.)  And you 
  are further correct that security through obscurity is not reliable.
  I should add that I have no delusions that everyone receiving email 
  from the various security mailing lists is a so-called white hat.

  Yes, security through obscurity is clearly not reliable.  Regardless,
  it can be a very definite (temporary) advantage to you and to your
  systems, and to the systems of others.  Please consider the effects 
  of a room full of your most untrustworthy users, and users that have 
  just told that the STOP command will bugcheck an OpenVMS system.  Do 
  you want someone to email all of your users with details of this bug, 
  before somebody (else) can get you a fix for the problem?  Probably
  not.  Like other forms of what I would refer to as security courtesy, 
  most folks would prefer that others not be impolite, and particularly 
  that general notifications of security problems not occur before a fix 
  can be obtained and loaded.  (Can we rely on courtesy?  Clearly, no.  
  Do we prefer it?  Most folks would, I expect.)

  Some might initially find it surprising that some folks actually 
  strive for security through obscurity -- where this obscurity is 
  deliberately sought.  There are computer systems configured to 
  misrepresent themselves to various network requests, responding 
  with the identity or the fingerprint of another platform.  Is this 
  a reliable protection?  Clearly not.  Does this defense stymie some 
  attacks?  Clearly yes.  Does the incompleteness of the defense defeat 
  its value?  Nope.

  Widespread publication of a security hole simply means that the "script 
  kiddies" learn about the bugs, and before it is possible to identify, 
  create, test, and then distribute a fix or a workaround.  Meaning that 
  you are exposed.

  Most any military organization has obvious security concerns and has 
  at least a few direct corollaries to this discussion -- corollaries
  to security through obscurity -- not the least of which involves
  shielding details of radio frequencies and of transmitter locations.
  Hiding frequencies and transmitter locations is obviously not reliable, 
  because once the AN/SPS-49A(V) radar lights up, everybody in the area 
  with a working radar threat receiver knows about it.  But that knowledge 
  might be just a little too late for somebody.  And thus obscurity can be 
  a very definite -- albiet clearly temporary -- advantage.  (Consider that
  you had the best search radar currently available.  Would you rather show 
  off its existence and its availability to your opponents, or would you 
  prefer to display an older pre-NTU AN/SPS-40 series radar until you really 
  needed to use the good stuff?  Tactics...  Tactics...)

  Would I prefer to send every OpenVMS System Manager full details of a 
  security bug or vulnerability, and as soon as it is identified?  Sure.
  (Folks here at Compaq work with CERT for just this reason.)  Would I
  rely on obscurity for my security?  No.  But could I justify my posting 
  details of an OpenVMS security problem into comp.os.vms for all to see, 
  and particularly posting details prior to the availability of a fix or 
  a workaround?  Um, no.  That would be, um, discourteous in the extreme.


