On Fri, 21 Dec 2001, Alan Greig wrote:
> > Here you are wrong.  Security hole bugs and their fixes were most
> > certainly posted to the TOPS-20 group.  That was its original purpose.
> Hmm, as you ran the mailing list I'll accept what you say even if I
> don't off-hand remember any being posted. Not that there were that many
> security problems - mostly system crashers as I recall and I don't want
> to suggest there were lots of them either!

There weren't many security bugs in TOPS-20, but there were some.

The one that I remember best was a truly nasty security bug which I posted
to the list on May 28, 1980.  I think that I discovered it, but perhaps
someone else called it to my attention; I no longer remember after 21
years.  The bug was that the privileged MSFRK system call -- perhaps the
most dangerous system call in all of TOPS-20 -- made the wrong check and
thus anyone could execute it. DEC put in the fix into development sources
on June 2, 1980, but who knows how long it took before word got out to the
full customer base?

> My point still stands though. It would have been one thing posting
> details to a controlled circulation mailing list and another posting
> them to "human-nets" for example. Or so I would argue.

That war is long lost.  If the VMS world hasn't had to face that reality
yet, it should; maybe then we'd hear fewer claims about VMS security.

There are several open mailing lists to discuss security bugs *and post
exploits of the bug*.  You're supposed to give the developer a heads-up
and a few days to make a fix and get it out before posting, but lots of
the kiddies are more concerned with counting coup than such niceties.
You can be sure that once a bug is posted, the script kiddie attacks will
start in a matter of hours.

Your VMS system may have a known gaping hole a mile wide, and you had just
better hope that everyone who is privy to that information guards it well.

> Is the TOPS-20 mail list still active btw.

Yes.

