Might I observe too that info-vax is getting to be a VERY high volume
group, mainly due to these speculations and replies to them. Can't some
of the speculations go into some other group and leave the tech stuff
in this one?

The pdp10 reminiscences are fun - for a while - but the technical fraction
of the content here has never been lower IMO during the 15 or so years
I've followed the discussions.

alt.vms.corporate.politics and alt.vms.conspiracy.theories might be
good names for groups in which to put most of the recent discussions.

If I have complaints about things VMS that are political I have Rich Marcello's
email address, and can find others for Mr. Capellas or various folks at HP
if I need to. 

Historically the mission of info-vax/c.o.v. has been much more technical
which made it much more rewarding to read.

Some of the topics of discussion could well become so again. What, for example,
are the major differences we might expect in IA64 versions of VMS? Surely
the data structures will get at least little tweaks in kernel mode, and I
have hoped for a few things in support of I/O layering which are difficult on
Alpha because certain data structures are already defined and in use by third
party packages and VMS engineering is justifiably loath to alter them.

Is third party boot going to get easier? Will anything simplify driver writing?

Some of these bits might be getting clearer in good old ZK3 about now, and it
would be nice to have hints. I figure that some re-layout of IRP, UCB, maybe
DDT, FCB, and so on might be needed just to get things clean on the new
architecture..not so much as was needed in the vax->alpha move, but a little,
and so the little bits I mention might be natural mods. Anyone who is an IA64
expert care to comment?

Anyone care to comment how updated caching maybe should look, work, be designed etc.?
(given, that is, that it is desired that most old code be usable with recompile
or translation)?

Glenn Everhart


