Not so.
The reason for the lack of buffer overflows is due to a number of factors.
One: VMS habitually has used descriptors to pass strings, which have sizes
passed. (Contrast windows NT and successors. Take a look at the ntifs.h and
you will find numerous input arguments which are null terminated strings,
some even which are arrays of such, terminated by a second null.These are
sitting like time bombs waiting to cause trouble, and will not readily
be removed by automated tools pulled from the public domain or internally
written that find gets() and friends. As a rule input string size is NEVER
available, and many of the APIs are security critical. VMS OTOH must be
searched long and hard to find any such cases, thanks to the insistence
on descriptors. It may have been a pain but it was pain with a payoff.

Two: The process that creates VMS asks about security and customer data, not
just by code review, but asks at investigation time, at design review time,
at function spec time, and at code review time. This makes it relatively
hard to slip things by. The culture insists on polish.

Three: Much of VMS has been to the wars, gotten badly hurt, and been replaced
so that much has changed since V1, V2, or V3. 

THAT is why VMS gets so few problems. If it were hit harder, a few more might
surface, but the "today's Nth critical security flaw" you see in Windows, or
the "this week's Nth flaw" that happens in Linux at times would still IMO be
rare, as it is with OpenBSD, for the same reasons: the people writing the OS
care deeply about getting security right and are damn good at what they do.


If you take code written by careful people watching out for one another, you get
a better result than you get with code written by careless folks working
separately. Less code, generally, but FAR less problems down the road. Nor
am I sure that over the very long haul you get less code, since ultimately
crappy code needs to be redone more.

The claim below amounts to "all coders are equal" in security results. But
some companies foster care, others foster carelessness. This makes coders
unequal, and we should be able to figure out which way works better for
security.
Glenn Everhart


