Despite Compaq's public declarations of Alpha's inability to maintain a
significant performance lead over The Good Ship Itanic, some pesky customers
still aren't convinced about the desirability of the migration.  But Compaq
has a story for them as well, just not a consistent one:  Itanic may be a
pig, but the Alpha team will miraculously transform it into a pig with flies
(er, sorry, a pig *that* flies...).

Kerry Main floated this rationale the day of the announcement and Mark
Gorham tried to feed it to Alphaman a few days later, but it then seemed to
disappear (I'd like to think in some small part because I spread it to
comp.arch and comp.sys.intel and Intel was not amused - perhaps HP as well).
But it's alive and well out in preferred-customer-persuasion land, as this
email I answered yesterday demonstrates.  JF may be right:  Compaq isn't
much interested in new Alpha system business or in many small existing
customers, but cares enough about a few important current clients to say
whatever it takes to keep them on board the sinking liner.

Anyway, you can be a fly on the wall listening in on our (slightly
expurgated, to remove identity clues and one private piece of information)
conversation, and conclude what you will:


> Hi Bill,
>
> ... I told our Compaq account manager in no uncertain terms
> how I thought about the infamous Itanic move. Of course he replied that
> everyone else was very happy etc. (specially management guys), and that
> we were the only ones unhappy. That wasn't true of course, but hey he is
> a sales person.
>
> But he was very quick to act, and today we had another guy who told us
> what is actually the case, and how it all came to this. He is a former
> DEC support guy, and he was refreshingly honest.

He may have believed what he said (a lot of people at Compaq seem to have
swallowed the party line as gospel), but the information he imparted is
dramatically inconsistent with the information I have received from EV8
developers now at Intel - and on the subject at hand, I know whom I find
more credible.

>
> I told him again my vision on the Itanic (didn't differ very much from
> your vision with regard to the technical side of things) and he could
> understand it very well.
>
> But then he gave us some more information about the whole matter.
>
> First of all he said that the whole marketing presentation of this move
> was done in good old Digital fashion, as stupid as it can be. As if we
> didn't know. :-))
>
> What happened was that the EV8 development team went to Capellas and
> said that without a lot more funding it would be impossible to keep up
> with the X86 class of processors (and possibly Itanium ?)

I very much doubt that any such mission to Capellas occurred - it sounds
much more like the spin Compaq tried to palm off as part of the June 25th
announcement that the decision had started with the Alpha engineers.  They
later amended this to saying that it originated with some of the *server*
(not processor) designers - and there's an interesting political tension
between the two groups involving the migration of much of what had been
server 'turf' onto the chip in EV7.

There was *no question whatsoever* in the minds of the EV8 team that they
would extend, not simply maintain, Alpha's current performance advantage
over Itanic.

Keeping ahead of IA32 processors would have been more difficult but far from
impossible, at least for Intel variants (Hammer might be harder to match):
EV7's on-chip memory control (with its dramatically improved bandwidth and
latency) will hit the streets next year with nothing similar from Intel on
any road map (32-bit *or* 64-bit), and while Intel will debut 2-way SMT soon
in IA32 (again, nothing on any road map for IA64) EV8's 4-way SMT should
have been a more than adequate response.  And in any event IA32 just isn't
(and shows no sign of becoming) much of a threat to Alpha's bread-and-butter
markets.

 We are
> speaking here in terms of raw processor speed of course, not the quality
> of the OS. Of course the intellectual capacity is there, but not the
> funding.

The funding *was* there, until June 25th, to continue the Alpha performance
road map that has been public for years and showed no sign of being
inadequate (or unduly expensive).  Again, I'm reasonably sure that your
informant was feeding you misinformation, though not necessarily knowingly.

 And indeed, the performance of the fastest X86 class processors
> at the moment is about the same as that of the fastest present Alpha's.
> Only now we have 1 GHz Alpha's, I had expected them at least 1.5 years
> earlier. Development is slowing down, and so is the performance
> advantage of the Alpha.

If IA32 were truly Alpha's competition, that argument might have at least
some merit.  But if a 32-bit architecture were considered sufficient in that
market, there would have been absolutely no reason for Intel to have
developed a 64-bit machine that shows no evidence that it will *ever* come
even close to catching up with IA32 on simple speed benchmarks (with the
possible exception of FP-style code, which is not the main source of Alpha
income).

>
> It was the EV8 development team who suggested joining forces with Intel
> to design a new processor.

No, it was not.  I don't know how tightly-knit the European DEC community
is, but if you know <name deleted> I think he may have additional inside
information from a source that I don't (he's another person who was
initially fed a load of crap but then discovered what it was from other
sources he trusted).

 This processor is now known in the Compaq
> presentations as the Itanium 2, and apparently it has almost nothing in
> common with the present Itanium, but instead is EV8 based.

More bullshit, I'm afraid.  This is another lie that Compaq tried to float
to make the decision easier to swallow, but it doesn't stand up to
examination (nor, incidentally, do I believe you will find *any* indication
from *Intel* that this is what's planned - so Compaq is asking for your
trust - hah! - while ignoring the signals from the architecture's owner).

First of all, *if* Compaq recognized that Itanic was the disaster it appears
to be and that Alpha technology would remain far superior, why on earth
wouldn't they have vigorously capitalized on this asset rather than handed
it over to Intel supposedly so that Intel could rescue Itanic with it?
After all, if Itanic *wasn't* destined to change the 64-bit landscape, then
Alpha would have remained as competitive (with any marketing) as ever, with
increased leadership clearly visible in the near future.

Second, I've received private confirmation from one of the transplanted
Alpha designers that my guesstimate of 2006 as the earliest such an
Alphabetized Itanic could appear is a good one, which puts it about 3 years
behind the date when the same technology would have appeared in EV8.  So
*even after having given the technology away* Compaq will now have to wait 3
more years to make use of it than would have been the case if EV8 had gone
ahead, while POWER4 reaps the rewards of lack of any high-end competition
and Hammer may start grabbing large chunks of the mid-range-to-low-end
market.

Whatever Itanic will come after Madison is far more likely to be an EPIC
architecture with Alpha-inspired tweaks than an Alpha architecture running
the Itanic instruction set.

 The
> instruction set will be different of course, but as I understand the
> overall design will be very much like the EV8.

One of Alpha's strengths was its instruction set, which was explicitly
designed to allow performance enhancements.  The design behind Itanic's
instruction set was based on completely different (EPIC) assumptions which
are less amenable to using Alpha-style performance approaches even if the
entire EPIC implementation is abandoned.

 Why they still use the
> infamous name Itanium for that new processor is unclear, but we all
> agreed that this again is very stupid marketing.
>
> The Alpha technology has not been sold to Intel, but it has been
> licensed. The Itanium 2 / EV8 team hasn't moved, but are still at their
> own desks at Compaq. The only difference is they have Intel badges now.
> But what's in a badge as Shakespeare might have said.

It's not the badge, its the architecture.  Alpha is an eagle, and Itanic is
a pig.  Compaq has just given Intel the means to streamline the pig and put
wings on it, but in the end it's still a pig:  all instruction sets are
*not* created equal (just look at IA32 for a cautionary example of how much
more effort it takes to make a poor one perform).

>
> So in essence your vision on the Itanium (1) is right, it is a piece of
> shit Compaq agrees. In fact Dell has stopped selling Itanium based
> systems as I read in a newspaper over here.
>
> Compaq however has so much confidence in the Itanium 2...
> (Don't publish this please,
> don't know if this is official already).

It sounds like the so-called 'golden blanket', and given that even if the
mythical Itanium 2 does appear it won't be before 2006 this is just more
smoke being blown to try to make an indefensible position palatable.

It is possible that some of the on-chip memory and multi-processor support
could be grafted onto the McKinley/Madison core by 2005, and there's some
indication that this may be what you are referring to as 'Itanium 2' (i.e.,
a processor core with absolutely no Alpha technology in it, and hence just
as much a pig as McKinley/Madison will be).  And while Compaq has been
promoting the fiction that it might appear in 2004, again this does not seem
to be consistent with private information from the transplanted Alpha team.

>
> It seems that at the Los Angeles presentation there were several very
> important customers that were very unhappy with the way Capellas etc.
> answered their questions. Only after these customers talked with
> technical staff they were satisfied that the Itanium move was ok. That
> again proves how poor the marketing is in this case.

No, it just proves how gullible people are.

>
> Compaq ... is visiting all important customers now to explain them
> what is happening. And sure enough there were other customers too who
> were planning to leave Compaq because of the Itanium move. But after
> this guy paid them a visit they changed their mind.

See above.

> ...  It seems to be sure now
> that HP UX and Tru64 will be merged to one Unix, even if the merger
> doesn't go ahead. The best of both (in Unix ?? :-)  ) will be in the new
> Unix.

Except that it will be big-endian, so even if the amalgamation is performed
perfectly the little-endian Tru64 people will be faced with a non-trivial
port.  Not to mention that the HP/UX kernel is a large, antiquated monolith,
which bodes poorly for its long-term future.

> So maybe things are not as gloomy as I (we?) thought.


