"Bill Todd" <billtodd@metrocast.net> writes:

> I'm ashamed to admit that I just can't recall the details with any certainty.
> I do seem to remember that angle-bracketed directories may have been a DEC
> standard (including both RSX and TOPS) until DCL came along and decided to
> move to syntax that didn't require use of the shift key, but don't remember
> dot-delimited version numbers on RSX at all (until we wrote RMS to parse them
> as an optional syntax).

(Square) brackets were always the DEC standard; Tops-20, and only Tops-20, got
angle brackets (AKA brokets) from TENEX, a BB&N alternative operating system
for the PDP-10 that DEC eventually licensed (as Tops-20 v.1).

I'd been using Tops-20 for several years before I learned that it would accept
[directory.name] as well as the native <directory.name>...

