Bob Supnik wrote:
> 
> With all due respect to the correspondents on this thread, the causes
> of DEC's demise are substantially more complicated than Ken Olsen's
> opinions on PCs and UNIX.  At the time Ken made his statement about
> home computers, microprocessors were in their infancy, and computers
> (in 70s dollars) were too costly and too unwieldy and unreliable for
> home use.  By the end of the 70s, when technology had made a personal
> computer feasible, Ken pushed DEC into building personal computers
> (although not IBM compatible PCs).
At the time KO had the meeting with Tandy, I was working on two
PDP8 efforts - one a hand held, one phoneline powered device.
There was also an 11 effort to make a device that was in the base of a
phone, with video and keyboard conneciton. I don't recall printer...
the idea was telco being a timesharing system, like TSS or online
systems...
> 
> The root causes of DEC's demise lie elswhere, in management response
> to changing business economics.  The computer industry changed from
> being a low volume business with high gross margins, to a higher
> volume business with lower gross margins.  DEC was unable to change
> fast enough as the business models shifted, or to find an appropriate
> balance point.  Sun exemplifies a company that implemented a tightly
> focused, higher margin model; Dell a company that embraced a high
> volume model.  Both have prospered.   DEC tried multiple strategies,
> at once, and failed.
I can only provide my analysis at the time, and the impetus for me
leaving 
dec.  On the hardware side, I percieved that there were two paths -
prior 
to PC intro, and after Apple intro - Large systems and chip design. 
We had national semi buidling a couple of variants of the 11, and trying
to 
take on that market space as a vendor, that failed by the way, and intel 
pushingtheir new chips, moto pushing  theirs, rumors of an INTEL version
of
the pdp11, MOS Technology - not MOSTEK - doing their micro..that sure as 
heck in the 6500 model was an 8bit pdp11 - producing stuff at prices
less 
than we could.  The culture was changing, and the path to a single 
product was written on the wall.  

The culture change, the impact of the Business side of what had been
an engineering enterprise, the impact of more competition - IBM coming
out with their series1, finally acknowledging minis were real...
and all that contributed to a path that led to the actions that flushed
dec.

> 
