I believe that some VERY late RSX11M or maybe just M+ releases would
accept decimal version numbers. For most of their history they did not.

Also the command language DCL looks to me to descend from CCL, Concise
Command Language, which appeared under pdp10 monitors circa 1970 as a frontend
to hide some of the command idiosyncracies of PIP and the other CUSPs
and was the conceptual ancestor (from what one can tell from outside of
DEC) of the commands in OS/8, its descendant RT11, and the DCL which got
put into RSX11M (which was well and truly much later; the initial console
was MCR).

Jim Downward did something called CCL also for RSX11M 3.1...a remarkably
useful and friendly piece of software...but the pdp10 thing was 10 years
or so earlier. Saved one from typing things like 
R PIP
*output <- input/switches

(where one used the character now used for underscore as a reverse arrow. That
was changed from the old ASCII 64 definition and led to use of < or = characters
in later OSs.)

The very earliest pdp10 systems had no highseg hardware and I believe just
handed out memory as a user process asked for it. The loader was used before
running a program and if I recall rightly one could SAVE a loaded executable
and then just run the .SAV file directly later. Linking to disk was not the
norm in the early days as I recall it. The first pdp10 I used had no disk
at all, used one of 8 DECtapes to hold the CUSPs. It had no trouble running
peripherals itself; the models that used pdp11 to handle peripherals were
later.

This probably preceded the naming of the old pdp10 monitor TOPS; in the old
days it was just "Monitor, Vxxxx". (Note too a certain fuzziness of memory
about these details after 30 years...)


