I'm 99.9% sure that the total blocks is the visible read/write area
(accessible to user programs if mounted foreign or to the file system
if inited and mounted as this one is.)  So it does not include
BBR's, diagnostic tracks, etc.

I think that on RZ (SCSI) disks with variable geometry (more sectors
on the outer tracks), the cylinders, sectors and tracks numbers
are synthesized (either by VMS or the disk, I'm not sure which) to
the smallest possible underestimate of the disk block size, to
minimize waste, but I don't think that applys to any SDI disks
(except solid-state disks!) and these are the true number of
cylinders, tracks and sectors.  The blocks reported is always the
product of the cylinders, sectors, and tracks.

VMS uses the cylinders/sectors/tracks to scatter the backup home
blocks across the platters, to increase the odds of being able to
recover something from a crashed disk.  This scheme doesn't work
if the numbers are fictitious, but I think they decided the extra
capacity was worth the very small risk of having a partially but
not totally trashed disk, and losing data that in theory could have
been recovered.  I think a recent thread on comp.os.vms mentioned
that they've abandoned this scheme, at least for SCSI disks, and
INIT just puts a couple of backup copies of the home block
somewhere near the beginning of the disk.

Reserved bad block replacement blocks are visible to the host;
I don't know if there are really n+1 sectors on each track, and
sector n+1 (i.e. sector 52) is the replacement block, or if there
is some other scheme.  Also, aren't there one or two cylinders
reserved for replacement blocks in addition to the diagnostic
cylinders?  My guess is there is a standard scheme, and the
diagnostic cylinders are 1916 & 1917, and the BBR cylinders are
1918 and 1919, but I don't know how to check that.

Hope this helps.

-- 
