For better or for worse, Mark Crispin is not a troll.  He has worked on
PDP-10 systems for many, many years and wrote many useful programs for the
10/20 community.

He also has a way of being divisive.

While he's probably baiting people in the newsgroup (and has obviously
grown more ornery compared to his prior posts from 16 years ago (see the
one that Alan Grieg dug up from 1984 via Google which was much more
balanced), I don't think he deserves killfile status.

I do not know Mark at all, have never met him, and disagree with several of
his positions.  However, I don't like seeing someone who is very much a
part of the PDP-10 family considered an ordinary USENET troll.

In the post that you decided was baiting by a troll, he posits a VERY
important question.

UNIX systems can often be made to misbehave, grant privleged access to
users who don't deserve it, and even crash by being handed more data than
they're expecting to handle -- the buffer overflow problem.  So can other
modern OSes and applications.  It's a pervasive, pernicious problem that
plagues much software developed today.

The most obvious one that comes to mind is just a few years ago when a
bunch of UNIX vendors, as well as Microsoft and Apple, had to issue patches
for a problem where ICMP (ping) packets that were larger than the OS
expected would cause crashes.

According to one paper published by the Oregon Graduate Institute of
Science and Technology, summarized in a C|NET article (see pointer below),
the buffer overflow was the biggest computer vulnerability of the 90s. 
UNIX had plenty of them; Windows and applications like Exchange are riddled
with them.  

The article is here: <http://news.cnet.com/news/0-1003-200-1462855.html>

Why?  Because the logic to painstakingly check the data being handed to
kernel and driver routines for sizing problems, data type mismatches, or
invalid content was left out or incomplete, especially in early
implementations.  Why was it left out?  Because it costs people time to
write these checks, they take up space in the code, and it costs CPU time
to execute them.  UNIX was initially implemented first on the DEC PDP-7 and
then almost immediately ported to the PDP-11.  Address space was at a
premium.  Significant error checking was a luxury.

Many of the older OSes, including Multics, TOPS-10/20, and also including
VMS, were designed by people who cared deeply about making sure the OS
would keep running and would stay secure.

Here's a pithy example of the difference.  The following quote is from the
Multics history site at www.multicians.org.  It's a snippet of the page
<http:www.multicians.org/unix.html>, in which Tom Van Vleck, one of the
longtime Multics designers, discusses the relationship between Multics and
its famous child, UNIX:

        "I was working for MIT in those days, and one thing I did was to 
        organize an MIT PDP-11 users' group and encourage them to look into 
        Unix. The idea of a free, non-vendor-supported operating system was new 
        to them. I invited Dennis Ritchie [one of the founders of UNIX] to come 
        up and talk to them. 

        "We went to lunch afterward, and I remarked to Dennis that easily half 
        the code I was writing in Multics was error recovery code. He said, "We 
        left all that stuff out. If there's an error, we have this routine 
        called panic, and when it is called, the machine crashes, and you
holler       
        down the hall, 'Hey, reboot it.'" "


Hence, buffer overflows and other nastiness.  This philosophy is by NO
MEANS restricted to Unix; it is very much evident in Microsoft-written OSes
and applications (like Exchange, IIS, and many others), as well as plenty
of other products.   It's evident when keeping to software schedules
determined by marketing expediencies is deemed more important than spending
the time to do a correct design and to test every conceivable failure mode.  
It's evident any time that developers decide to not bother writing error
checking code because it's "not interesting" and are allowed to get away
with it because doing so will impact schedules.


This is a long reply, and has wandered somewhat off the topic, but the
point is still fairly clear: Mark Crispin is NOT a troll, even if he is
fanning the flames a fair bit.  His points _do_ have merit.

John Francini, user of TOPS-10 from 1978 to 1990 (system
programmer/developer from 1982-90)

