>similar problem.

Possibly.  I didn't follow the evolution of IAS.  I also know that
the people behind IAS cared about it working which also makes a huge 
difference.  

MOst of my commentary is dealing with the first years of VMS when
evolution of the operating system was tightly controlled based on
the implementors' idea of what it should be rather than what their
customers needed.  IMO, that [respect of customer input] is the 
key to producing a robust operating system.  I have been told
by customers that VMS is wonderful for their use (this is
1990s flavor of VMS).  I am bringing up all of this old shit 
about TOPS-10 because I see the same damnedable thing happening 
to a well-matured operating system that happened to us in the 80s.

And, children, a well-matured operating system is A Good Thing.
This in no way implies that all innovation is done.  Quite the
opposite is true.  It implies that innovation can be done quickly,
efficiently, and robustly.  Getting an operating system into 
production is not merely coding.

/BAH

