I would also add that we had technology failures - we were not able to
respond quickly enough to the emergence of RISC processors, and our
bread-and-butter VAX business went into decline because we could not produce
a competetive VAX processor.  We also had organizational failures, where we
allowed the 800lb gorilla organizations - like VMS - to prevent (or slowed
down) the innovation needed to get to the Next Thing.  We also had delusions
of grandure - we believed that our competition was IBM, and were killed by
the hungry guys with the simpler, cheaper solutions.

If we had been able to push the VAX performance, as Intel has pushed x86...
If we had been able to disconnect VMS from the VAX when we were first asked
to...
If we had been able to move to Prism/Mica...
Things might have been different.

Of course, you can add about 40-50 other "If we only had..." things to this.

But I agree that our shotgun, unfocused strategies - hoping that at least
one would work - didn't.  And once a company turns from growth strategies,
to a cost focused financial management...  eventually you fail, or are taken
over.


