In article <francini1026-ya02408000R2112010135240001@news.ne.mediaone.net>,
John J Francini <francini1026@mac.com> wrote:
>        "We went to lunch afterward, and I remarked to Dennis that easily half 
>        the code I was writing in Multics was error recovery code. He said, "We 
>        left all that stuff out. If there's an error, we have this routine 
>        called panic, and when it is called, the machine crashes, and you
>holler       
>        down the hall, 'Hey, reboot it.'" "

It's worse today. At least UNIX has error *detection* code, and code is
rewritten now and then to allow recovery from more errors, and there is
an actual system call interface where security and overflow checks on
system calls can be made.

In Windows, there is no single system call interface. Every call that
crosses a protection boundary has to invent its own protection mechanisms,
because it's all based on regular shared library calls rather than traps.

And, of course, instead of having 30 or 40 system calls (less on earlier
systems, more on later) you have tens of thousands of calls that run with
elevated privileges that really need to exhaustively check all their
arguments.

