Perhaps if more of the investment in the tech boom was focused towards
competing with Microsoft IN A DIRECTED WAY, then there'd maybe been
more competition today.

The Larry Ellison network computing thing ended up being stillborn
after too much meddling and too much demoing.   Additionally, it architecturally
had some nits that continued to make PCs superior (however horrible
they are.)   There were wasteful political factions involved also, which decreased
efficiency from an otherwise lean and mean development effort.  I
was in the middle of it, but looking on the outside as a work-at-home
person also.

There were numerous other network computing-type competitions, the
FreeBSD/Linux/others competition were useful, but not providing adequate
full integrated products for desktop.   (I targeted my FreeBSD work for
server loads, not desktops), but even that wasn't sufficient for much
server work.   Even if FreeBSD had more time spent towards it, like
other thigns, the free stuff ends up being a herding cats syndrome.
