layering, black box (but heavyweight) APIs, and bundling.   They have infinite
assets, and have purchased many of the bigger names in computing (not
that they always get it 'right.')   They even have some of the more 'interesting'
DSP-types.

Fortunately for me, I am NOT an anti-Microsoft person, but also don't like
them very much.   This made me an advocate of contrary viewpoints, because
I believe that it should not be a crusade against Microsoft, but an education
of the user base about the alternatives.   Nowadays, for the desktop, the
alternatives aren't as interesting relative to Microsoft as they used to be.
Even I am using Win2000 for casual stuff (I still prefer UNIX for more
complex tasks, and have a good sized network for such work/experimentation.)

