I suspect Alan was referring to hardware capabilities.  But I'm curious
about your reaction to IAS, since it was built on top of RSX-11D - which had
real-time capabilities and shared significant architectural roots with
RSX-11M and VMS.  This ties into another of your posts where you seemed to
suggest that having a good time-sharing environment was in some way
incompatible with having a good real-time environment (so I won't raise the
question there separately).

VMS did over time take significant steps toward supporting decent
time-sharing use, and in fact from the beginning was *not* positioned as a
'hard' real-time system (in contrast to RSX and RT on the 11).  And while
trying to be most things to most people indeed increased its complexity
(another criticism raised elsewhere), IIRC IAS suffered from a very similar
problem.

