There were plenty of people in the 1980s and 1990s doing their best to sell
desktop Unix systems.  Sun didn't get in the big-honking-server business until
after buying the Cray intellectual property from SGI.  Unix was trying hard for
the desktop market; Windows kicked its ass.
>
>The only thing that the number of Windows systems vs. UNIX systems proves
>is that the desktop market has more individual units than the server
>market.  Well duh.
>
>> Of course this doesn't make it technically superior; the idea was to show the
>> flaw in Mark Crispin's logic, where he explained that the market had proved
>> that Unix was technically superior to VMS.
>
>Another non-sequitor from you.
>
>VMS and UNIX are in the same market.  People had a choice.  They choose
>UNIX, and did so even when VMS was bundled with the machine.
>
>There's a long list of things in which UNIX is technically superior to
>VMS.  Portability.  Shell vs. DCL.  Better process management.  Much
>better Internet support.  Piping.  The vast library of UNIX tools.  The
>list goes on and on.

Thanks for beginning to make evaluatable claims.  I wasn't actually interested
in a Unix-vs-VMS pissing contest; I was just objecting to an assertion that
the market success had proved anything about technical superiority.


>I was an employee at Stanford when Stanford got one of the first VAXen.
>It came with VMS, but it was converted to UNIX as soon as they got a tape
>from Berkeley.  I still have an account on that system, although it has
>different hardware.
>
>Stanford's CS department is one of the top in the world.  They've never
>allowed market considerations to dictate what hardware or OS they will
>run.  Historically, they have run some strange systems in their pursuit of
>technical excellence.  Why did they choose UNIX?  Stanford EE and KSL are
>also among the top in the world.  They too choose technical excellence
>over market considerations.  They're also UNIX shops.  The Stanford
>student computing facility was a DEC-20 clust, and later became a UNIX
>cluster.  Why didn't they go with VMS?
>
>Just how many VMS systems are there at Stanford?  Do you need more than
>one hand to count them?  How many VMS users?  How many thousands of UNIX
>systems are there at Stanford?  How many UNIX users?

(My laboratory, SSRL, is part of the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center.
SSRL has about fifty VMS systems; I think there are about 75 more on the
SLAC site.  There are a few on the main campus; not many.)

>
>If you contend that Stanford picked a technically inferior operating
>system, I know numerous current and former employees at Stanford who will
>say that you don't know what the hell you are talking about.

I'm a current employee at Stanford and I say I do know what I'm talking about.
So there.  (However, I wasn't arguing about whether Unix was technically
inferior; I was arguing about whether the market proved anything.  My actual
position is that different operating systems have different strengths and 
weaknesses.  VMS generally works well for me and for the needs of my 
employers.)

First, this is a different argument than the one I was objecting to, so even
if I bought it it wouldn't invalidate my original point.

Second, it's a blatant appeal to authority rather than any effort to validate
your point.

Third, there are other plausible reasons for Stanford having gone with
something other than VMS.  Did the people who were running the DEC-20 cluster
get so upset with DEC over the end of the 36-bit lines and the ascendance of 
VAX that they swore never to run VMS?  (I'm under the strong impression that
at least one of those people went off, eventually, and co-founded XKL, which
argues for at least a strong emotional attachment to the 36-bit systems.) Did
Digital salesmen try to talk them out of VMS systems in the mid-90s?  Did other
vendors offer tremendous deals which Digital wouldn't meet or beat for VMS?
Did Stanford think it would be more responsible to train students in an
operating system that had multiple vendors behind it than in one with only one
vendor, and that one sending inconsistent messages about how long it would be
around? Those aren't strictly technical, but those are factors that could well
have had an impact on the decision.


-- Alan


