On 19 Dec 2001, Carl Perkins wrote:
> "TOPS was great

Yes, TOPS-20 was great.

> so when they
> killed it I moved to Unix

As did the entire Internet PDP-10 community, every major university and
research organization in the US.  Many of the commercial users went to
IBM.

> because I was mad at them and VMS
> stinks anyway"

No.  We moved because we had to move.  UNIX was the only viable
alternative.

There was nothing -- and I do mean nothing -- of attractive value in VMS
in 1983.  It would have taken years of work to get VMS up to speed, and
that would have been in the face of VMS developers who proudly proclaimed
that they would not allow any such thing.  Nor was there any Internet
support in VMS at that time.

It took years to get UNIX up to speed, but the UNIX developers were
willing to listen, and on occasion did something we asked them to do.
UNIX already had a proven Internet track record, and in fact had been
selected as DoD's standard Internet OS (which at that time meant a very
great deal).

There were even early UNIX desktops back then, the vanguard of the attack
of the killer micros.

Finally, UNIX did not have the disadvantage that killed TOPS-20.  UNIX was
not under the control of a proven unreliable vendor.

-- Mark --

