On Thu, 20 Dec 2001, Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr wrote:
> In number of licenses sold or computers deployed, it seems very likely that
> there are orders of magnitude more Windows systems (counting all variants)
> than Unix systems (counting all variants).

Non-sequitor.  The primary market for Windows is on the desktop, whereas
the primary market for UNIX is in servers.  Each have made inroads into
the other market, but the dichotomy continues to exist.

The only thing that the number of Windows systems vs. UNIX systems proves
is that the desktop market has more individual units than the server
market.  Well duh.

> Of course this doesn't make it technically superior; the idea was to show the
> flaw in Mark Crispin's logic, where he explained that the market had proved
> that Unix was technically superior to VMS.

Another non-sequitor from you.

VMS and UNIX are in the same market.  People had a choice.  They choose
UNIX, and did so even when VMS was bundled with the machine.

There's a long list of things in which UNIX is technically superior to
VMS.  Portability.  Shell vs. DCL.  Better process management.  Much
better Internet support.  Piping.  The vast library of UNIX tools.  The
list goes on and on.

I was an employee at Stanford when Stanford got one of the first VAXen.
It came with VMS, but it was converted to UNIX as soon as they got a tape
from Berkeley.  I still have an account on that system, although it has
different hardware.

Stanford's CS department is one of the top in the world.  They've never
allowed market considerations to dictate what hardware or OS they will
run.  Historically, they have run some strange systems in their pursuit of
technical excellence.  Why did they choose UNIX?  Stanford EE and KSL are
also among the top in the world.  They too choose technical excellence
over market considerations.  They're also UNIX shops.  The Stanford
student computing facility was a DEC-20 clust, and later became a UNIX
cluster.  Why didn't they go with VMS?

Just how many VMS systems are there at Stanford?  Do you need more than
one hand to count them?  How many VMS users?  How many thousands of UNIX
systems are there at Stanford?  How many UNIX users?

If you contend that Stanford picked a technically inferior operating
system, I know numerous current and former employees at Stanford who will
say that you don't know what the hell you are talking about.

