On Thu, 20 Dec 2001 00:58:15 -0800, Mark Crispin
<mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> wrote:

>There's a long list of things in which UNIX is technically superior to
>VMS.  Portability.  Shell vs. DCL.  Better process management.  Much
>better Internet support.  Piping.  The vast library of UNIX tools.  The
>list goes on and on.

All of the above is now in VMS. Even PIPE functionality on the DCL
command line. The goal of VMS engineering in recent years has been to
make porting to VMS from an arbitrary Unix no harder than porting to
any other Unix.  About a decade ago DEC had a half-baked attempt to do
this with the VMS Posix subsystem but this time it's being done right.
As an example the Mozilla browser beta releases come out for VMS
simultaneously to the Unix releases. We're now at Mozilla 0.9.6 with
the functionality of Netscape 6.

Although occasionally Unix developers break the build on VMS that
happens with other Unix variants as well. Work continues in this
area..

Many of the GNU tools build out f the box on VMS and there are ports
of various Unix shells. Apache is supported by Compaq on VMS as the
recommended web-server.

As to PIPE functionality that's been in VMS for years now.

$ HELP PIPE


PIPE

     Executes one or more DCL command strings from the same command
     line. The PIPE command enables you to perform UNIX style command
     processing, such as command pipelining, input/output redirection,
     and conditional and background execution.


$ pipe show user/full | search syss$input burnett
 BURNETTG      XXXA1  BURNETTG MA      20893E1B  TNA6469:
 BURNETTT      XXXA2  208902A4 MGL310  208902A4  TNA6394:
$

You can argue that  the VMS implementation is a bit verbose if you
wish but not that it isn't there.

Even TCP/IP integration is much, much better and still improving. I
have an identd server written entirely in DCL as, under VMS's TCP/IP
support, you can associate a DCL .COM file (which can do the work
itself or run an arbitrary program) with a socket just as you can
under Unix inetd. I even know of someone who wrote a basic NNTP server
in DCL. Bet I could write a basic IMAP server in DCL given enough
inclination.

