On 19 Dec 2001, Dan Riley wrote:
> IPv4 always included 24 bit subnets[1]; some earlier ARPANET protocols
> did use 8 bit host numbers, but IPv4 had less in common with those
> protocols than DECnet phase IV or V shared with phase III, and phase
> III had limits at least as bad as those early ARPANET protocols.

Work on the transition from 8-bit ARPAnet addresses to 32-bit addresses
was underway in late 1970s.  I implemented the first PDP-10 32-bit address
("long leader") NCP in early 1978, although UNIX apparently had it first.
By 1980, there were enough systems that were only accessible using long
leaders that I am fairly sure that short leaders had died out by then; any
stragglers would have been rendered extinct by the January 1, 1983 TCP/IP
transition.

By the way, the addresses in question had more in common with MAC
addresses than with IP addresses.  There was no IP layer in the NCP world,
and the 40 bit NCP header had no addressing.

So, how well along was Phase IV and V in 1980?

