I know more about it than I ever wanted to know, but I don't hate it.

> I have had the distinct non-pleasure of having to port a large application
> to VMS.  I knew that it was going to be a pain when I started, but even
> then I was awestruck at how reliably VMS made the wrong decision whenever
> there was a clear choice between the right thing and the wrong thing.

How do you mean that? I don't understand what choices VMS makes for you.
Do you mean the optimization of the C compiler? Hey, there's a reason
for years the optimizer in ANY C compiler was considered to be black magic.

I worked with the original author of a largish database application that
ran on a KS10 and we converted it to a VAX-11/780 w/ VMS 4.1, but in MACRO
Not C. This was in '83. It was a pretty easy conversion, except for having
to retype all the code. But that only took a few months. Otherwise, it was
a breeze. Probably the one thing that saved me was TECO was available.
Actually, I kinda lost momentum somewhere in the middle of the project and
I was taken off of it - now that I think of it, I was kinda overcome with
the tediousness needed to accomplish anything. I was very excited about
it at the beginning, but after the theoretical part was done, the actual
coding had to be done - and on VMS it was killing me. DDT was sorely missed.

The thing that impressed me was the library of manuals. Unless each feature
was described at least 5 times, there were just way too many features :)

> The less said about the VMS C compiler (and the C library!), the better.
> It was worse than the early versions of the Microsoft C compiler, and
> that's saying a lot.

I recently wrote some very intense multi-threaded (POSIX) code to communicate
on sockets. I moved it to VMS(VAX) and the biggest things I had to alter were
file names and permissions. Some other touchiness about files but I don't
think there was anything really hard to move. This is VMS 7.2 w/ whatever
DEC C came out at the same time. As a matter of fact, moving it displayed
a whole set of bugs that I would never have seen on Sparc or Intel.
This was in C, SparcWorks for Sparc, gcc for Intel (and demo Forte), and
DEC C (6?) for VMS.  

aak


