Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> writes:

> >> > It was a stated goal by Hutsvedt(sp?) to ensure that anything
> >> > done on the PDP-10 would never be done in VMS.
> >> Hence VMS' demise became my stated goal.  I was one of many who refused to
> >> develop software for VMS, and instead improved UNIX's lead so that VMS
> >> would never catch up.
> >...which is just as immature an attitude as that reported of Hustvedt.
> You might call it immature but if DEC suddenly killed the operating
> system and hardware you loved and earned an income from after making
> supposedly irrevocable promises under NDA about the future then
> *added* to that by insisting that certain features would *never* be
> ported to VMS, I find it perfectly understandable. 

Understandable emotionally? Yes. Understandable rationally? Never. That
DEC's management was immature and didn't act rationally has nothing to 
do with it. Otherwise, "he started" would be a valid defense in court, which
in almost all cases it isn't.

I worked at the time with TOPS-10. Maybe TOPS-20 was soooo superior to
TOPS-10, but the latter certainly wasn't superior to VMS. And what really
gets me in Mark's original comment was "improve Unix's lead". Come on,
really. I started to have to use Unix a few years later than VMS, but at 
no time did I consider a contemporaneous Unix having a lead over VMS. Even
now, I can't say Solaris is so much better than VMS V3.

As I said, immature: cutting of your nose to spite your face.

> When was it that VAX Mail finally had the ability added
> to mail user@hostname without some third party binary hack and third
> party mailers? Around 1995.

I used VMS daily until 1990. At that time, only universities in Germany had
a connection to BITNET (for which VMS support thourhg Joiner Associates was
very adequate). At that time, viable third party solutions became available.
Would you rather have had VMS destroy the market for these ISVs by offering
the same bundled with the OS?

> A decade or two after TOPS-20. 

It was irrelevant to almost everybody a decade before.

> Not send out all customers a letter telling them of the "non-goals" 
> which would never be ported as they did.

Do you have a copy of that letter, or could paraphrase what those non-goals
were?

> >I do believe this has more to do with the lack of an affordable TCP/IP stack
> >of good quality early enough than anything else.
> 
> No that was just a symptom of the DEC stupidity of the time. At one
> point the Internet was dominated by DEC then they decide to kill off
> these machines and justify the lack of an IP stack by saying "VMS
> systems are not normally connected to the Internet" Or do you think
> DEC just accidentally produced an unaffordable crap IP stack?

Not at all. Maxim #1: "Never attribute to malice what can adequately
be explained by stupidity." In this case, DEC bet on ISO-OSI has being
the next big thing in internetworking. You do remember the official
requirements for government contracts in the US and parts of Europe in
that regard, don't you? And from a technical point of view, it was clear
that TCP/IP, with its design starting point of a network of no more than
254 nodes that were implicitly to be trusted, wouldn't be able to support
a large "internet" efficiently and safely. (That's not to say that a lot
of OSI, especially the user-visible stuff, isn't abominable because it was
designed by idiots, but that's another issue.) Nonetheless, I consider it
a supportable business decision at the time. What you can criticize is that
DEC, and especially the VMS group, didn't notice the wind turning, and didn't
react quickly enough to the change. And what they then produced (UCX) 
certainly suffered from NIH syndrome, because apparently nobody bothered
to consult their in-house TCP experts. And the same happened with VAX C.
(That's a classic example of going by the letter of a (not yet existant,
in the case of C) standard instead of looking at industry practice, IMO.)

	Jan


