Sorry Jan,
but at the time there was internal competition to produce better 
quality products. There were CPU design competitions even within
the groups - I don't know how this was in 10 land in the 70s, but 
with in 11 and 8 land it was true.  The concept/design stage
versus the production stage was somewhere between 2:1 and 6:1 at any
given time.  The same was true of periph design, maybe a 4:1 ratio.

With the advent of Vax efforts - the result of some years of study
and experimentation that involved competing designs - the sense
of constructive friction or competition was ingrained.

Senior management was doing their job - they grew up in the same 
environment.  Mid 70s or so, the influx of mid level managers from
other cultures (meaning non-dec) eventually had an impact. I know of
a number of folks from Honeywell, RCA, IBM, retired Govvies, etc
who did not get the culture and found ways to squash it to the 
deteriment of Dec andthe benefit of Digital - there is a subtlety
in my use of the two labels that I think others have and can 
articulate with great color.

I have used the term purple plague - that was the term we in
8 land called the 11 efforts (that spawned vaxen).  It was 
characterized, in my mind, by a large number of troops under a
strict maangement structure, marching to deadlines and promises
they did not keep.  This is a generallity, and is not true in
all cases.  There were some exceptional efforts, but we did get
infested with MBAs and move from a bunch of engineers and techs and
software wizards into a model of a dysfunctional IBM.
my two cents.
bob


