Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> wrote
in news:y4k7vjsduq.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de: 
> "Who, me?" <who@me.com> writes:
> Yes, I quite agree. But be honest: At the time DEC bet on OSI, would
> you have bet that part of the company on TCP?

I can't honestly say what I'd do, because that point in time was early in 
my career and I didn't have the breadth of knowledge to make that kind of 
call.  But DEC's decision was consistant with their corporate culture and 
the corporate culture of similar companies like IBM.  Networking was a 
profit center, and they were going to do everything they could to not kill 
the goose laying the golden eggs.  OSI was perfect for this: you could 
claim to have a standards-compliant protocol, yet not interoperate with 
competitor's equipment!  

I saw the same logic in place at both IBM and DEC.  I was a UNIX network 
developer at IBM then I left and went to DEC in the early 90s.  Both 
companies had large entrenched proprietary (i.e. non-TCP/IP) networking 
groups (Raliegh, Littleton) that were very protective of their proprietary 
product lines.  Both spoke of TCP/IP as a toy protocol incapable of 
supporting large networks.  I thought DEC was a bit further along in the 
evolutionary path: IBM still though it was a good idea to spend large 
amounts of mainframe cycles supporting routing protocols, and would speak 
of how many mainframes they sold whose primary purpose was to support 
network activities.  DEC seemed to realize that most folks were buying 
their systems to run scientific and business workloads and didn't want to 
spend lots of their cycles supporting routing protocols and would offer 
scaled-down systems to support the network activities.

Neither seemed to see the TCP/IP revolution breaking, but from what I could 
see Raliegh accepted it much earlier and began pulling in all TCP/IP 
activities that they could get their hands on, wheras Littleton stayed in 
denial pretty much right up to the end when they were sold off to 
Cabletron.

Dave


