My $0.02 of the DEC I saw in the early 90s after leaving IBM was that the 
inmates were running the asylum.  I think DEC needed more MBAs, or at least 
the right MBAs.  In that era, IBM was #1 and DEC was #2, and now DEC is now 
dead and IBM is still the largest computer company in the world.  IBM had 
lots of MBAs that were making sure the techies were building things that 
had a target audience that could and would pay the amount of money it took 
to keep the whole thing running.

Basically DEC had a totally unreasonable, unworkable business organization 
and no one seemed to notice or care.  Almost everyone involved was a techie 
and only cared if they were working on cool stuff instead of actually 
working on providing solutions to the customer's current needs and 
providing the teamwork neeeded to keep the company afloat.

Pretty much all of the folks I worked with who were honest would admit that 
DEC's solutions were overpriced, and that the company put the customers 
through torture when trying to actually buy a DEC system or get a bug fix 
or enhancement, and that we had very little software available that 
customers could use to solve their problems.  All those things were just 
someone else's problem.  All they wanted to do was work on cool things.  
There were no MBAs around to remind them that in the long term that just 
doesn't work.

So, you end up with something like Tru64, which has the best clustering of 
any UNIX but near-zero market share, which is going to be killed in favor 
of HP-UX which doesn't have much technical merit but lots of market share 
and applications.  And VMS, which seems to have a business case if you look 
at the numbers just right, but can easily be killed by whatever corporate 
parent ends up owning it when they decide that it's cooler and cheaper to 
just ship whatever Bill Gates calls enterprise software this quarter.


