In article <3C1FA846.56662CF6@iee.org>, "a.carlini" <arcarlini@iee.org> writes:
> 
> I missed out on TOPS-10 and TOPS-20 (just!)
> but given the plethora of emulators now
> available, it looks like I'll get a second
> chance. Which features is it that VMS lacks
> but TOPS-10 and/or TOPS-20 has? I don't doubt
> that there are some or many, it's just that
> I have no idea what they are. Or are we
> talking mainly features that were missing back
> then but are around now?

   An older, 2 mode, OS design that was popular at about the time it and
   UNIX were invented.  Really painfull API to the OS, unreachable from
   many high level languages.  Forks instead of processes.  A loader
   that really was:  you loaded all your objects into memory, then you
   could run them, or you could dump them back out to an executable in
   a file.  A lot of security holes.  The inability to separate the
   concepts of an authorized user and a disk directory.  No predefined
   RMS (eventually COBOL and FORTRAN groups realized they were both
   doing record management and came up with a common RMS).

   If you were used to thinking this way you might not want to learn
   anything new or different from what you'd been doing.

   The guys who designed VMS were doing something new and differenet,
   virtual memory and 4 modes, but were willing to base the OS design
   on a tried and proven concept (RSX).





