"Who, me?" <who@me.com> wrote in message
news:Xns917C6BE8E5675whomecom@199.125.85.9...

...

> DEC's decision was consistant with their corporate culture and
> the corporate culture of similar companies like IBM.  Networking was a
> profit center, and they were going to do everything they could to not kill
> the goose laying the golden eggs.  OSI was perfect for this: you could
> claim to have a standards-compliant protocol, yet not interoperate with
> competitor's equipment!

Depends on how you define 'corporate culture' (and whether what I would call
it had changed by then).  The culture up until the early '80s was definitely
*not* anti-interoperation - in fact, interoperation was the cornerstone of
DEC's networking business, since most people interested in networking had
some IBM equipment they wanted to include (IIRC this was often how DEC got a
foot in the door and then later some its own systems).  Nor was it
characteristic of the OS portions of the company (how could it be?  they had
to interoperate with each other anyway).

It did seem to become an element of corporate *strategy* (as distinguished
from corporate culture) around the time when the 'one OS to rule them all'
attitude appeared at high levels.  But what I'd call the culture didn't
change at the same time.

- bill





