Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:
> "Who, me?" <who@me.com> writes:
> > OSI was perfect for this: you could claim to have a standards-compliant 
> > protocol, yet not interoperate with competitor's equipment!  
> 
> I'm sure interoperability was a requirement for those government
> contracts.  And in the early years, TCP/IP was so loosely defined
> (especially compared to DECnet and SNA, which both had a largish
> number of third-party implementations) that regular interoperability
> parties were conducted.

It is remarkable how many "things that made me go 'huh?'" you managed
to pack into one sentence.

IP and TCP have been defined by RFC 791 and RFC 793 since 1981, before
DECnet phase IV was released.  I can't remember any time when I knew
of more DECnet phase IV (or later) implimentations than TCP/IP.  If
there hadn't been many different implimentations of TCP/IP, they
wouldn't have needed interops!

People still hold interops for relatively new (and sometimes not so
new) protocols.  Even if the spec is perfect, implimentations never
are.  If DECnet didn't have interops, it was because the "right"
behavior was effectively defined by a single vendor's implimentation,
not because of any attributes of the spec.

Interoperability testing is simply good engineering practice, and
interops are a convenient way of testing interoperability among many
different implementations.  I don't believe the mere existence of
interops demonstrates any deficiency of specification.
-- 
