On 19 Dec 2001, Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> And in the early years, TCP/IP was so loosely defined (especially compared
> to DECnet and SNA, which both had a largish number of third-party
> implementations) that regular interoperability parties were conducted.

How soon the children forget.

The reason for those interoperability bakeoffs was to guarantee that there
was interoperability, and to fix any ambiguities in the specifications.

There were none in the OSI world.  They did not care.  That's why TCP/IP
implementations interoperated, and OSI ones did not.

And I can tell stories about DECnet interoperability problems that are not
suitable for squeamish eyes.

