The theme this week seems to have been on business strategies and that's
got me thinking about what Compaq have been doing and why they may have
been doing it.


Maybe it all comes down to a single idea - Compaq are simply not
innovators.

Sure Compaq have Alpha, Wildfire and lots of nice storage gear - but
these were Digital's innovations.  Sure Compaq has iPaq - but that is
just an improvement on the Palm Pilot or even Psion ideas.  Sure Compaq
has Evo - but that is pretty standard components in different packaging. 

The last truly innovative product that Compaq developed might have been
their first product, right back in 1982.  Compaq's only semblance of
innovation came about by developing ideas that came from Digital (which
was one very innovative company).  In this area their marketing has let
them down yet again and no-one knows about things like Wildfire and
Galaxy.  Ask 10 people who do not work in VMS to tell you what they know
about Wildfire systems and you'll see what I mean.

Dell has showed what innovative sales methods can achieve.  Compaq's are
stuck back in the 1980's or 90's with all their sale people and
distribution channels.  What's worse, Compaq's strategy of telling the
customer what they should buy, instead of telling the customer all of
their options is old marketing at its worst.

Compaq just doesn't innovate in product, marketing, software or
services.  Inspirational Technology ?  Give me a break.  All they are
doing is being another "me-too" company, for all intents and purposes
identical to everyone else in the industry.

Compaq decided to dump Alpha and adopt IPF - and be just like the
competition.  Their future compilers will be from Intel - just like the
competition.  They aim to be a service company - but there's already a
whole bunch of Unix and NT service companies and Compaq will be just
like the competition.

 If there is no obvious differentiator, then all that Compaq can do is
compete on price - but reducing your profit margins is no way to make a
good income (as Compaq PCs have consistently proven).


Termination of Alpha was a blunder of massive proportions.  The high-end
Unix servers will now have little to distinguish them from the
competition.  Himalaya and VMS are rather different but if Compaq
continues to be as silent as a grave on these products, then these
differences are not important.

Terminating the NT-on-Alpha project was probably also a bad move, but
with NT being pretty unstable at the time, the performance advantage of
Alpha may have been lost.

On the subject of the maybe-merger, if Compaq (and HP) continue the same
path of just being one of the crowd, using more or less standard
components, and looking like just another clone, then I expect no
significant improvement in their performance either as a merged company
or separately.


If they are to survive and prosper, Compaq needs to become innovative
and they need to introduce tools and services that are of value to their
customers.

Kerry Main recently mentioned that he was working with an environment
involving Java on VMS.  This is a beautiful example of what can be
done.  The performance, reliability and security of VMS supporting
industry-standard software.   So very similar to how Compaq got
started.  It meets a need like no other product can.


Unless Compaq really desires to be lost in the low-margin mediocrity of
being just one of a number of similar companies offering similar
services, it has to get innovative.  It has to take a hard look at what
it can create that new, that is different, that is useful.


It can start by scrapping the sales segments and opening up the field to
any operating system for any customer.  The sales people don't even have
to know every product in details, they just have to be prepared to give
the customer information about all possible options.  (Second thought -
this idea doesn't even cost them any money !)

Just expanding the markets could easily throw up ideas and applications
that Compaq have never considered.  Just as importantly it will create a
good differentiator in the services and consulting area.  Unix and NT
people are easy to find, but Himalaya and VMS people are something
else.  Extend the market for the systems and the consulting work will
soon follow.  Suddenly they are not so much a me-too player any more,
but they have something different to offer, something starting to be
innovative.


Shouldn't be that difficult, should it ??


John McLean


