In its class (the desktop), yes.  The primary competition was Macintosh.
Mac had an early lead over DOS and early versions of Windows, but failed
to build on that lead.  Apple pissed off a lot of its customers too.
Windows 95 was the death knell.  I suspect that Mac OS X will turn out to
be too little, too late (not to mention the fact that it's basically just
NeXTstep redux).

MS has expanded into the server market, where it is encountering much more
stiff competition.  The UNIX world is much less complacent than Apple was,
but it would be well-advised to keep MS' history of relentlessness in
mind.  It seems that they have; although the Linux invasion into the
desktop market probably will never be a serious threat to MS, it's a smart
move nonetheless since it forces MS to expend resources to guard its
desktop monopoly.

MS has effectively no presence in other markets.  Which is probably a good
thing.

