Peter da Silva wrote:
> RSX used DDn:[nnn,nnn]file.ext;v universally. I've never seen any
> RSX program using <> or a . for the version. The RSX legacy in VMS was
> the mapping from DDXn:[nnn,nnn] to VOL:[nnnnnnnn], IIRC.

I've never used RSX but the RMS-11 User's Guide
(for RSX-11M+ V2.1/RSX-11 V4.1) has the file spec
rules listed in Appendix A. It lists both
[group,member] and <group,member>. It also
has ;nnnnn for version numbers, states that
nnnnn is in octal (!!), allows for ;0 and
;-1 (but not the general case of ;-n). It
also states that for compatibility with 
other versions, you can use a . instead
of a ; (but that seems to be frowned upon).

Whether RSX picked up that usage from 
elsewhere or not, I cannot say (this
manual is dated April 1983).

Antonio


