Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:
> And from a technical point of view, it was clear that TCP/IP, with
> its design starting point of a network of no more than 254 nodes
> that were implicitly to be trusted, wouldn't be able to support a
> large "internet" efficiently and safely.

Odd, that description sounds to me a lot more like DECnet than IP.
IPv4 always included 24 bit subnets[1]; some earlier ARPANET protocols
did use 8 bit host numbers, but IPv4 had less in common with those
protocols than DECnet phase IV or V shared with phase III, and phase
III had limits at least as bad as those early ARPANET protocols.

[1] In fact, in RFC760 a 32-bit IPv4 address was always an 8 bit
network number and 24 bit local address.  The addition of different
sized class b and c networks was one of the big changes from RFC760 to
RFC791.
