> attempt to expand that Enterprise market.

The "uninformed" part is many portraying Compaq as a company built on
pushing out mass market PC boxes rather than understanding that what made
Compaq successful was engineering leadership in their marketplace.

It is in Compaq's nature, and as the article pointed out its likely path to
success, to provide technology leadership.  I believe management has come to
understand the folly of competing with Dell on Dell's terms.  What Compaq
will do is return to its business model and position itself as the
technology leader in servers.  That is good for Tru64/OVMS/NSK.  Once the
QuickBlade and Storageworks technologies are fully implemented in the next
30 months Compaq will be in a very strong position to provide technology
leadership across all server markets and make money doing it.  What caused
Compaq's current problems is not WinTel but becoming obsessed with Dell and
trying to play Dell's game (which they are weak at) rather than playing
Compaq's game.

What that article added to my thinking was this - I have always believed
companies that attempt to charge a premium price for technology by providing
technology leadership always get into trouble as the market matures.  What
that article correctly pointed out is there an emerging sweet spot in the
server market and that Dell's business model can't fill it without breaking.
At least for the next 4-5 years Compaq's business model should work in that
sweet spot.  Until that sweat spot becomes commodity technology Compaq can
win.

There are two things that I hope everyone takes away from that article 1)
That Compaq's business model is based on technology leadership 2) That
technology leadership _only_ works if you can create something that the
market values.  The mistake that is often made is to assume that technology
leadership by itself (i.e. technology for the sake of technology) will win
the day.  It only works it the customer values that difference.  If the
customer doesn't value it  they won't pay the premium price.  Customers make
that call based upon a business decision and not a engineering basis.  Just
because the engineers appreciate its doesn't mean the folks who make the
business decisions will appreciate it...






