The VMS group was afflicted with what was known as "VAX jealousy": the
knowledge that they had an intrinsically inferior operating system
compared to what came before.  The only way they could have their place in
the sun was to destroy the PDP-10 utterly.  And they succeeded.

The company let them because they sold many more VAXen than PDP-10s.
Never mind that this was mostly because the PDP-10 was outrageously
overpriced compared to the VAX, and that as the years went on DEC widened
the pricing gap.

The company also let them because at the highest levels of DEC, a decision
was made to scrap all its architectures except for one.  "One company, one
architecture."  First the 18-bitters were killed in 1977; then the
12-bitters; then the 36-bitters in 1983.  The 16-bitters were killed too,
although it was done in a much more subtle manner (for all I know they may
still be making some form of PDP-11).

The PDP-10 folks bear some of the blame; they allowed themselves to become
aloof and passive.  The disease was well underway by 1977, and by 1980 the
death knell had started.  We started wearing self-delusional T-shirts with
slogans such as "I don't care what people say 36-bits are here to stay."

-- Mark --

