In article <Pine.NXT.4.43.0112180720020.29894-100000@tomobiki-cho.cac.washington.edu>,
Mark Crispin  <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> wrote:
> I read the original GNU paper.  Linux is *not* the operating system
> described in that paper.

They actually got Hurd to the point where it would run... I've seen it
at Usenix, but it was years too late to make a difference. Not only is
it hard to write an OS (even with a bootstrap system and a good model
at hand), but a microkernel design is doubly hard. Linus chose a
traditional monolithic kernel for Linux rather than a microkernel for
a reason: it's very difficult to get adequate performance from a
microkernel design: every server is a potential single-threaded
bottleneck, so you either have to multithread it, split it into a "top
half" and "bottom half" that buffer and cache uncompleted transactions
between them, or create a herd (a sub-hurd?) of servers around to take
turns handling requests.

Minix of course was a poster-boy for this problem, but that's not Tannenbaum's
fault: it was intended to be a teaching tool, and adding all that complexity
would have hurt its utility there. Other pure microkernels have had similar
problesm... for exmaple, on the Amiga the file system was often a bottleneck,
though they managed to avoid the problem in most of the other servers.

So the FSF was fighting an uphill battle on all fronts, I don't blame them for
failing given the magnitude of the task they set themselves. I do agree they
could have been a heck of a lot more diplomatic and statesmanlike about Linux
right from the start.

