In article <jjru1u81gffl4654alh48soo11sa3ieotq@4ax.com>,
Alan Greig  <a.greig@virgin.net> wrote:
>Linus Torvalds has stated that without Gnu there would have been no
>Linux. Maybe someone else would have done it later but...

Linus has also said that if Net/2 had been released a year earlier, there
would have been no Linux: he would have used BSD instead. He's said a number
of things about his motivations, and he's never been unwilling to share the
credit for his work or to acknowledge the competition.

But I think he's mistaken. If there was no Gnu, then people who were working
on GCC wouldn't have just vanished. They would have put their energies into
one of the other compilers out there. If there was no Gnu, the people working
on duplicating the UNIX software tools wouldn't have just vanished: there were
dozens of other groups doing the same thing as the FSF, they just didn't have
Stallman's drive behind them.

I don't think that things would have been dire in a world where the FSF
didn't exist. All the other people would still have been working on free
software, and would have coalesced around other cores as they reached
critical mass.

By the time Linus got tired of Minix, and decided to bootstrap his new OS,
there would have been other tools available.

