Jeff Killeen wrote:
> 
> There has been a group of posters in various newsgroup continuously taking
> shots at Compaq based on what I consider to be based on wholly uninformed
> positions about Compaq's history. 

... (snipped)...

Sorry Jeff but I think the authors are barking up the wrong tree.


This is not 1982 (?) when Compaq developed something novel and very competitive (but
still profitable) against the slow-moving IBM.

This is 2001 when people like Dell have developed a business model that is very
competitive against the slow-moving Compaq.

This is not a time when Compaq can assemble its own PCs with its own components, but
a time when most of the PC industry has moved offshore to contract-assembly
companies, and work with mass-produced componentry from specialist companies.

The authors suggest that Compaq should go back to being innovative.  To a small
extent it has worked but the iPaq sales are not enough to rescue the whole Compaq PC
division. Losses of 485 million dollars this year are not smart, nor is a measly
profit of less than $250 million last year.

The idea of iPaq was fine but it doesn't take long for the competition to appear. 
Here, for example is some info about the Toshiba Pocket PC e570 which looks very
competitive (
http://www.zdnet.com/supercenter/stories/overview/0,12069,531282,00.html )


To flip the problem over, Compaq bought some very innovative stuff from Digital and
about from the Storage stuff, they have done very little with it.  As I showed some
time ago, diverting just 5% of the money to pay for PCs into the "Enterprise" sector
could easily return about 15 times more in income.

Compaq didn't always make such poor income from PCs.  I haven't checked the figures
but I believe that the return on investment for PCs between 1994 and 1997 was about
12% but in 1998 it fell to 6.3%.  By contrast Dell's income went from $944 million in
1997 to $1460 in 1998, a jump of 56% !  (Dell's business model was far superior
because it kept the costs right down.)

Instead of becoming innovative with its new purchases, Compaq had a closed mindset
that kept it bound to the PC market regardless of how non-productive it was (and
is).  The brilliant innovative drive of Compaq founders (Rod Canion, Jim Harris and
Bill Murto) has long disappeared.


I had hoped to find what happened to these guys but time is against me and I'll give
you two quotes from Rod Canion (as reported in Robert X Cringely's "Triumph of the
Nerds" on the web at http://www.pbs.org/nerds/part2.html)
 
Rod Canion:
  And the way we did it was by having low overhead. IBM had low cost of product but a
lot of overhead - they were a very big company.
 ....
Rod Canion:
  And by keeping our overhead low even though our margins were low we were able to
make a profit.


So what are are we left with and where should Compaq go ?

Right now we have a company who is floundering in a dead market and seems reluctant
to push the envelope and get serious about real Enterprise computing.  They have
dumped a perfectly good car just to catch a ride on the Intel bus.  They have decided
to hang onto the coat-tails of Microsoft rather than go it alone.  They want to be an
"industry standard" box maker, to run with the pack, which of course means to be as
mediocre as everyone else

If Canion, Harris and Murto were not afraid to be different, not afraid to be
inventive, then why the hell can't Compaq do so now, albeit several steps up the
computing level ?  They should never have dumped Alpha but they should have pushed it
as hard as they could, and then some more.  Instead we got to see Kamikaze Compaq in
all its glory (?), first with Alpha and then with HP.  Pity really...


That's my 2 cents worth (plus tax)...


John McLean


