news:3C17EA30.28F5A5B6@starpower.net...
> No typo in the title, I got the RA81 running on my VAX 4000 and mounted
> it and it looks like it has VMS 3.x on it!  Looking around on it I find
> files with names that start with "starlet" and "rsx" and "rms".  (Now I
> know why the 4000 gave "?" when I tried to get it to boot from the
> disk!)
>
> When I look around in [SYS0.SYSUPD] the .com files seem to be for
> version 3.x.  How can I tell for sure what version of VMS it has?
>...

One thing that might work is the command;

HELP /LIBRARY=newdisk:[SYS0.SYSHLP]helplib.hlb SHOW SYSTEM EXAMPLES

and look at what version number the examples show. If you see 3.7 in the
examples then you know that this is at least that new, if you see 3.4 (or
something else) then you could still have 3.7, but not 3.3. You might have
to use [SYS0.SYSCOMMON.SYSHLP] instead of [SYS0.SYSHLP].

You can also look to see if you have any file names on the disk that exceed
9.3  characters (DIR newdisk:[000000...]%%%%%%%%%%*.* and DIR
newdisk:[000000...]*.%%%%*). If there are any long file names then the disk
is post V4.0.

You can also tell us the date of SYS$SYSTEM:DCL.EXE, someone might know what
version you have based on that.

You can also try downloading the disk image to a PC and then use PicoVAX
(www.charon-vax.com) to see if a MVII will boot it. Check the download page
at www.charon-vax.com for a tool you can run that will create an image of a
VAX disk on your PC's drive. (I forget, was the MVII supported pre V4.0?)

Those are the only place I can think of where you can see a human-readable
version number now. I know the version number is also in
newdisk:[SYS0.SYSLDR]SYS.EXE, but at what location I can not tell you. You
can try a SEARCH newdisk:[SYS0.SYSLDR]SYS.EXE V3. and see what you get if
you want.

Hope something here helps.

Peter
