Connor,

You're far better off running an operating system for which there are drivers
for power management.  The HiNote VP765 is yet another one of the hardware
orphans in an industry now well known for dropping hardware support in the next
release of the operating system.  Your most likely choices are Windows 95, 98
and NT 4.0.  See if the Compaq/Digital website has the drivers you need for one
of the above, and install the corresponding operating system.

As a side note, one wonders who is calling the shots with operating systems
here.  It may not be only Microsoft making the decision to change the driver
interfaces from operating system to operating system.  It's the changed driver
software interfaces which cause drivers to break and not function when installed
in a later operating system.  The hardware manufacturers have a vested interest
in broken software driver interfaces, because if people want to use an older
hardware product with a newer operating system, well... they can't.  The choices
are to remain with an older OS with support for the hardware in question or to
throw away the "broken" hardware, replacing it with soemthing that works
properly under the new operating system.  Either way, consumers and enterprises
both lose out, and a lot of hardware perfectly suitable for a given purpose gets
scrapped.  This approach lends a new meaning to  planned obsolescence.

Of course, ex-DEC hardware is a special case.  As soon as Compaq completed the
DEC deal, all the DEC Wintel machines were goners... Ben Myers 
