A & B were probably spins that got pulled after tape-out and release to fab.

It's not uncommon to release to fab while still writing and executing new
simulation routines. Because you're never really "done" with simulation when
working with a chip that large, there's plenty of risk to manage as best as
possible, but schedules almost always demand you go out earlier than the sim
team would like...

If they yank it early enough they can duck a big chunk of the fab NRE, come up
with whatever fixes they need to correct the malfunctions, bump the revs,
generate new tape, and go back to the fab again - even while writing even more
simulation routines to bang against the tape...

Stressful. Very stressful...

/daytripper (Are there any *old* microprocessor designers? ;-)


