Jeff,

Interesting!  My knee jerk reaction is as follows:

1. As far as the desktop is concerned, and desktop computers make up the vast
majority of Wintel computers being sold, the business computer market (i.e. the
market for computers used for business, not for pleasure or consumer-oriented
pursuits) is somewhat stagnated, and best characterized by near-zero growth and
replacement of units too infrequently to satisfy the name brand players.  Why is
this?  Many users within corporations and enterprises have all the computing
power they need in computers in the 400-500MHz range.  So why replace computers
which are perfectly adequate for the usual office suite of stuff plus email and
some internet browsing?   In addition, the total cost of ownership of a computer
includes the SOFTWARE, and the latest full-featured MICROSOFT office software is
prohibitively expensive.   So is XP, especially with its onerous and cumbersome
licensing provisions and Passport.  And, yes, add in the cost of non-Microsoft
XP-compatible upgrades for anti-virus, backup, CD-burning, and just about any
other software that delves into the operating systems knickers to run.  (Of
course, I am disregarding the power users who may be the only ones within the
corporation or enterprise with a legitimate need for the fastest computers
possible:  Software developers, engineers doing heavy duty analysis, graphic
artists, etc.)

2. Except for IBM, who have the smarts not to compete on price in the ridiculous
consumer computer market, all the other name brands (including Dell!) are
killing themselves to compete on price in the consumer segment, which is
perceived as the only high volume market segment left.  The name brands are
attempting to sell boxes to serve as home entertainment stations, replacements
for your stereo, TV, and various gaming consoles.  Only as a home entertainment
station can they justify the speed of the newer computers.  They are all
dragging themselves thru the mud, because when you compete on price, something
has to give.  Quality of components, quality of assembly, warranty provisions,
and on-going support all suffer considerably.

3. The Compaq brand in the consumer market segment consists of a sticker placed
on a box assembled someplace where labor is really cheap and so are components.
(I got a Compaq motherboard the other day, looked up its part and found that the
board was actually made by Gigabyte for Compaq and loaded up with a Compaq BIOS.
I flashed the board with the off-the-shelf Gigabyte BIOS, to uncripple it, and
make it user friendly, not engineered to be used by cretins.  And let's not
forget Compaq's drive of choice for the Presario a few years ago: the Quantum
Bigfoot, a hard drive design based more or less on production of defective disk
platters.)  Guess what?  Just about every other name brand consumer-oriented
computer is made the same way.  All junk.

4. As far as I am concerned, Compaq can abandon the consumer segment.  The
writers of the article got that right.  The implication here is that Compaq
would experience some contraction in revenue as a result, not good for the stock
market analysts and touts.  But if Capellas thought with his brains instead of
his groin, and stopped thinking "My **** is bigger than yours", he would make
that sort of sane decision.  Fill in the word "company" or any other suitable
noun for **** above.  Any word you choose would be the equivalent of any other.
And, of course, the Compaq board of directors would also have to go along with
this decision, and everyone would end up with worthless stock options as
Compaq's share price plummeted.  None of them have the guts to make this sort of
decision.

5. The ridiculous part of all this is that for all practical purposes, Pfeiffer
(Foofer, whatever his name was) and Capellas and Co. have pretty much demolished
the money-making and well-regarded DEC service organization, and other
worthwhile and profitable operations which did not fit Compaq's strategic
direction, whatever that is.  How do the article writers propose that Compaq
rebuild and refocus its service organization?

7. Compaq effectively wiped out DEC's excellent engineering, too, in favor of
Compaq's less-than-excellent engineering, pulling a few star ex-DEC engineers
into Compaq engineering.

8. But I'll tell ya, until you get to the heavy duty 8-and-up CPU systems,
servers are becoming commodities, too.  So Compaq has precious little room to
innovate thru engineering, whether it be ex-DEC or Compaq engineers.  And it
takes major league R&D to come up with the 16-, 32-, 64-, etc. CPU servers, far
out of balance with the revenue realized from selling them.  The current
industry trend to divvy up the workload among a rack full of 1U servers does not
help the cause of complex multi-processor servers.

9. The DeskPro computers have always been well-designed and thought out.  Same
for the Wintel "Workstation" line, altho they are too darn proprietary for me.
But there's hope for Compaq here.

10. Frankly, until someone comes up with a major breakthrough idea, I don't see
much of a chance for Compaq to excel in engineering its server or DeskPro lines.
And I can't see them rebuilding the service business in a timely manner.  I do
not see a lot of hope at all for Compaq.  Greed and tunnel vision seem to be
doing them in, much the same way it happened to DEC.

Like I said, just a knee jerk reaction, not a well-conceived treatise by an
academic.  But food for thought and discussion... Ben Myers



