Either that, or actually *sell* business quality desktops. Something like a
beefier Multia or a compact form factor DL360.

The high end unit:

On the front, two slots for those nice low profile removable drives under a
locking cover, over a low profile laptop-style CDROM and floppy, plus USB and
sound jacks and a PC-Card slot.

On the back, ethernet, SCSI, video, mouse, keyboard, and serial port.
Duplicate USB and sound jacks. And a lock that lets you pull the whole
motherboard out of the slab the way you could on the Multia.

Inside, at most one riser that can fit one PCI card, or one ISA card, room
for one IDE drive.

Not a bloody iPaq/Evo legacy-free netcomputer. A real PC that you can load
up with some beefiness if you need it.

The low end unit skips the SCSI and SCSI drives, and the riser. As a result
it's about 1.5" high. Same motherboard otherwise. Maybe a couple of PCMCIA
slots for PC-Card expansion.

Build it reasonably solidly, and price it fairly. We'd buy it.

