In article <9uu2qi$pil$1@ronco.freenet.columbus.oh.us>, "Robert Schaefer" <rschaefe@gcfn.org> writes:
:My machine has processors from different sources, with
:different serial numbers and eeprom versions.  I know I can change them, and
:it boots just fine (aside form the error msg) as is, but is there any
:advantage to one eeprom version or another?  Should I just upgrade them both
:to the latest version, or is it tied to a hardware revision?  

  I'd use the most current version of the firmware, but there have
  certainly been a few cases were new firmware requires a hardware ECO.

:Is anyone keeping an archive of the various eeprom versions?  

  Compaq generally only stocks the most current, but -- where it
  matters -- an archive copy can usually be retrieved.

:Also, will different
:class processors work in the same computer?  

  Not together.  Upgrades are often possible, but sometimes an upgrade
  will require a certain revision of an I/O controller or backplane, etc.
  (IIRC, one of the upper-end VAX 6000 series platforms had specific
  requirements in this area -- the VAX 6000 series model 500? upgrade 
  had a minimum revision of the backplane required.  Again, IIRC.)

:As in if I pop two '400 cpus
:into my 6320 will I get a bastard 632420 with possibly only two processors
:eligable to be the primary, or will I get a non-booting box?  

  Most Alpha systems require the same processor generation and the same 
  MHz for correct operation of SMP.  Don't mix them, in other words.

  The VAX 6000 model 400 processor modules are roughly double the 
  performance of the model 300 processor modules -- 3.8 vs 7 VUPs.
  I'd retire the model 300 modules.

:Another
:sort-of related question-- is it possible to make a bit-identical image of,
:say, a TK70 tape; something like `dd' with unix?  I'd like to save the
:eeprom image I end up overwriting just to keep the bits from fading away.

  There are dd ports around, and there are tapecopy tools and such.

:One last question before I quit bugging you...  Will one hobbyist PAK boot a
:cluster, or will I need a PAK for each member?

  You will will need to replicate every PAK in every license file
  present on each system, when you have multiple licensing databases
  present within a cluster.

  A list of questions would be easier to parse and to respond to than 
  is a stream-of-conciousness paragraph.  No offense is intended.

