Hello folks:

Well, I rewrote my RQDX3 emulator with internal packet queue management on
my VAX emulator.  I set up two disk units - RD54 on DUA0: and RRD40 on
DUA1:.  That is software-based emulation, not hardware.  I tested
two operating systems - OpenVMS 7.2 and NetBSD/vax 1.5.2.

On OpenVMS 7.2, I sucessfully booted OpenVMS into my VAX emulator from
RRD40 disk drive (CD-ROM drive) directly.  I successfully booted it
through SYSBOOT to the banner of stand-alone backup (welcome OpenVMS
message).  OpenVMS sucessfully completed RQDX3 initialization and
scanned all units.  Whoa.  Before I recently implemented GUS with MD_NXU
support on RQDX,  I tested it but OpenVMS thought that RQDX3 has
zillions units!  Then, I implemented it.  OpenVMS succesfully scanned
all 4 units and found two units available/online.

However, after initialization and unit scanning were completed, OpenVMS
sent a GUS packet to Unit #0 again and again forever. It looks like
that it caught endless loop.  It happened each several seconds.  Also,
it sent the DAP packet to Unit #1 because it want DAP (Determine Access
Paths) information from RQDX3 emulator about the boot disk (DUA1:).
But, RQDX3 replied the NOP (No Operation) packet back to it.  When it
completed RQDX3 initialization, it should print "PLEASE ENTER DATE
AND TIME..." on the screen.  It acutally did not print that. :-(
Does anyone have solution with that problems?

On NetBSD 1.5.2, I successfully booted NetBSD/vax into the VAX emulator.
It prints some information about initialization processing.  It
mentions that it sucessfully completed RQDX3 initialization and
scanned all units that are online or available.  No problems...
However, it caught the machine check trap after printed "root
file system type: ffs".  It reboots again and again with the
same results.  I am figuring that out now.

Thank you!

-- Tim Stark

