"Those who do not remember their past are condemned to repeat it."  Think of
us as the "historians" of computing, or at least one hall in that vast
edifice.  Some on this list are actually continuing to use the Olde Beastes
in production environments, for various reasons.  But for myself, I enjoy
being able to support an artifact or two from those simpler days, and at
least informally educate others in the brief but intense history of
computing.  Even if no one else cares, I do, precisely because I "cut my
teeth" on those machines and will always have fond memories of them.

You say that Italians love their history, but that "computing does not
conjure up the same kind of memories. It is there to be used and discarded
after it has served its purpose."  Perhaps computing is simply a disposable
tool to you, but to me and others, it is an end in and of itself - which is
why we have newer and better technology, because we are the people who
created it then, and create it now.  I work in the software industry with
tools that were beyond our dreams twenty years, even ten years ago.  But I
can make that comparison because I have that perspective of years in the
industry; my small collection has provided a living history lesson to no
small number of people of my acquaintance.

I "cling to the old technology" because without it, my PDP-11/34 won't run.
:-)  But I'm writing this post on a 1.2GHz Athlon machine with 512MB of RAM,
running Windows XP Professional.  (Let's see, the 11/34 supported 256kB, so
that's a factor of 2,000....)  I'm not living in the past, but I can go into
the other room and visit it - UNIX 6th Ed. running on nearly original
hardware.

"Dark ages" of computing?  Not so, those were days of exciting innovation -
just like today.  If anything, I'd say we face darker times today: the Intel
architecture has virtually eliminated all meaningful competition, not
through technical superiority, but through buyouts and lawsuits.  Alpha?
Dead-ended.  PowerPC?  Marginalized.  MIPS?  Long gone.  (And not for lack
of software support; for instance, Microsoft provided the Windows NT
operating system for each of those processors, until they were no longer
supported from the hardware perspective.)  In its prime, the Alpha was a
clearly superior processor to contemporaneous Intel chips; imagine if
development had continued as assiduously on that line as on the Pentium....

With all due respect, your characterization of those of us who run DEC as "a
dog's owner taking it to a taxidermist after it has passed and still
throwing it sticks" is wide of the mark; my "dog" still "fetches" (and
stores!).  And when I crank up that 3kW-consuming Beaste, it even keeps my
feet warm.

-- Ian, running UNIX 6th Ed. on a PDP-11/34a


