You can put DSSI devices (drives or HSDxx's) on any of the internal DSSI connectors.

The DSSI terminators are at each end of the bus.

The VAX's I have (4000/100, 105A and 106A) do not have a DSSI bus that ends at a drive.  If it did the
bus would need to be terminated at the drive, using terminating resistors on the drive, or by not
installing a drive but instead connecting a terminator in place of the drive.  (Some internal SCSI cables
have a terminator at the end to avoid the problem of needing to enable the terminator on the drive.)

If you trace the DSSI bus you should find that one end of the cable connects to the DSSI controller under
the lower drive plate.  The DSSI bus is terminated at that point.    The cable goes from the controller,
through the drive mounting area, where the cable has connectors attached to it, and out to the external
DSSI connector on the rear of the system.  The connectors for the drives are in the middle of the DSSI
bus, not at the end, thus no termination is need at that point.  If you didn't have any external DSSI
cables connected to the system you would install a DSSI terminator on the external DSSI connector.
Since you have an external drive box it has a DSSI terminator somewhere in it.  If it's a R215F box it
has two DSSI connectors behind the front panel, and one will have a terminator installed.  If it's a
B400x box it has a terminator built into it, somewhere.

On some systems, like the 4000/105A and 106A, where both ends of the DSSI bus are connected to external
DSSI connectors on the back of the system, the bus does not end at the controller.  Instead, the cable
continues from the controller to the second external connector.  There is no terminator on the controller
in those systems.

