I'll second that emotion.

In our environment CPQ severly lacks credibility.  Their actions
over the last year have shown, beyond a shadow of a doubt, that
"betting the business" on CPQ would be irresponsible.   I won't
even touch on the attrocious decline in hardware support quality
since the CPQ takeover of Digital, or the clueless sales drones
that wouldn't give us quotes for Alphas because they wanted to
pitch Proliants.  I'll just reiterate what _I've_ run up against,
recently.

We are still running VMS for stock market stuff, but that is in
the process of being ported (to another OS and HW platform).  We
_had_ most of our number crunching and portfolio management
running on VMS, but that has been ported with the end date slated
for year end.  

I had an opportunity to get the company I work for to consider
VMS for a "very big project", or VBP, for short (so sue me, I
like three letter acronyms that start with V).  I argued long and
hard to explain that VMS could provide the scalability, fault
tolerance and performance that they would be hard pressed to get
from any other platform and OS combo.  

I convinced them that they didn't need "VMS people" for the VBP,
which is the usual argument from the developer types, since I
could provide them with SAS, C, Java, Weblogic, Apache, Perl,
Perforce... The Works!  (with the exception of unix shells, but
most of the commands they'd be using could be 'symbolized' to
look just like a unix shell, and it wasn't a deal breaker
anyway).

Then the Alpha bomb dropped.  So much for the time I spent
porting some of their applications and showing them how they ran
faster on a lowly DS10 compared to their other platforms.  "Yep",
they'd say nicely, "you convinced us that Alpha was faster and
would probably would have been so for the forseeable future, and
that VMS was the superior OS to base it on, but that doesn't seem
like the case now."  "Besides," they hrrumph'd, "Compaq seems to
have a credibility problem, didn't they say a while back that the
Alpha was good for another decade?"

"Um, yeah, I showed you that processor comparison white paper and
letter from a CPQ muckity-muck a while back", suddenly wondering
why the AC had stopped working.

"But VMS on IA64 will still be rock solid!  You won't have to
invest in those multiple vendors with barely proven products to
get the multi-site data center, data coherence, and fault
tolerance that has been in VMS for well over a decade!", I
countered.

"You can get VMS on IA64, now?", they said to me with arched
eyebrows.

"Um, well, no.  But Compaq has said that they have started the
port"

"But we need it now.  Also, since HP is buying CPQ, what
guarantees that VMS will be ported, more or less survive?", now
given the look that is usually reserved for the "spare change"
people on the city streets.

At that point I realize that point it is futile to continue
arguing, because they'd just start making circular motions with
their index fingers pointed at their ears if I continued.

But then again, what do they know.  They just produce the
software that manages one quarter of the world's wealth.  


