Quite a lot of them.

The EECS undergraduate computing facility at UCB in 1980 was a single
PDP-11/70 with 2M of RAM and three figures count of terminals multiplexed
to shared lines running all over the campus. It was still usable (for
1980 levels of usable) with 35 users actually logged in, and woudl continue
to run (albeit painfully slowly) with 70. It was also being actively used
for UNIX kernel development during this time, and a good bit of 2BSD was
created on it.

NT (Windows 2000), given enough hardware, is a decent desktop. Not great,
but it's competant enough at the job that I'm not permanently pissed off
running it on my laptop like I was with Windows 95... though when it ate
itself over Thanksgiving after staying more or less stable for almost a
year (far beating any DOS-based Windows box I'd ever used) I did say a few
things that would have been embarassing to have overheard... :->

