> Is there a reason for the layout of the new extracts?

The "old site" was prepared using DECUS 10BACKUP and DUMPER run
by a jury-rigged set of DCL scripts, all on VAXen running OSU HTTPD.
A complete unpack of all the tape images takes about 72 hours at
this point, even with a farm of VAXes in a cluster doing the
extractions.

The "new site" uses read20 and 10backup (Phil Budne's
latest versions) under Linux, with perl taking the listing
output, formatting it into HTML, and serving it up via Apache.
A complete unpack of all 150-odd tape images now only takes about
15 minutes, plus another 15 minutes to index the 50000 unpacked
files (total of around 2 Gigabytes) via ht://dig.  The "new site" is
still experimental, meaning that I haven't worked much at all on
nicely formatting the results (other than the nice formatting
that ht://dig does when you use the search form.)  Keep your
eyes open for rapid improvements :-).

Of course, the timings I give above aren't entirely fair, since
I'm comparing an Athlon 1600 XP+ against a bunch of underpowered
late-80's Microvaxes.  I'm never one for throwing the baby out
with the bathwater, but if I can make the site more maintainable
for me by moving to some more modern tools I'm all for it.

After I'm satisfied with the "new site", the old site will become
old-pdp-10.trailing-edge.com and linger on with few or
no updates.

Tim.


