:Bob Supnik <bsupnik@nauticusnet.com> writes:
:> I spoke to Steve Shirron, who authored the RQDX3 firmware.  There
:> never was a CD on the RQDX3; CD's were always on SCSI controllers.
:
:Not always.  The early ones such as the RRD50 were on dedicated non-SCSI
:interfaces.  I think they looked to the host like an MSCP device, but
:I don't have one so I don't know for certain.

  I am aware of no CD-ROM devices on the RQDX3 series controllers. 

  The earliest CD-ROM disk drives I am aware of on Q-bus systems -- the 
  RRD50 series -- used the KRQ50 (M7552) controller.  The KRQ50 presents
  the PU/DU interface to host and application software -- the RRD50 disk
  drive will configure as a DUc0: device.

  The RRD40-DC could also be connected to and operate on later-revision 
  KRQ50 controllers.  

  RRD40-AA and RRD40-DA were SCSI, and were in the embedded and standalone 
  enclosure configurations respectively.


