 >   platforms looks to be a solid one and a good decision.  But I have
 >   likely been established as an apologist, so my opinions carry little
 >   weight in the discussion.)

You've put it very well.  Everything will be based upon hindsight, since
Compaq has committed themselves to a course without any backup or 
foresight.  Let's forget the 'apologist' title for a moment, and ask you 
(if you can publically) to explain why it's a 'solid and good decision'.

There are some, possibly many, that think IA-64 will crater.  IA-64 and
Alpha are direct opposites of each other in at least one way.  Alpha is
dedicated to being efficient.  IA-64 tries to be a bit of everything.
Anything put on an IA-64 chip that is not dedicated to performance will
be wasted resources, with respect to performance.  Competitors with the
same (for sake of comparison) resources, with everything dedicated to
performance, will be ahead of IA-64, which will be carrying an extra
load, which will do nothing for 64 bit performance.  Without invoking
additional differences such as power usage, heat, EPIC, and such, all
else being equal, IA-64 will not be able to compete.

 >   The work on the Alpha EV7-class microprocessors and on the platforms
 >   based on these microprocessors continues -- this discussion is largely
 >   a review of what happens when applications need an upgrade from the
 >   EV7-class platforms.

Yep!  And the concern that if it's perceived by customers that no such
upgrade exists or is being planned, they will desert VMS.

 >   The alternatives I see to the currently announced plans involve the
