 >   another (current or future) microprocessor platform.


If I was in VMS engineering, and I was faced with a stupid decision made 
by management that I could do nothing about, and had the sole 
consolation of funding to make VMS as hardware independant as possible, 
then I would grab this one escape route and do my best with it.

As for platforms, it looks like Power4 is doing well.  I have no idea 
how well it would mate with VMS.  Should IA-64 crater, and Compaq or HP 
or whoever not want to lose the profitable VMS business, changing course 
to another platform will be an option.  But, there will be harm done to 
the public perception of VMS, losing first Alpha, then having IA-64 
being inadequate.

I don't know why Compaq had to announce discontinuing Alpha before the 
IA-64 port was completed.  It scares off the customers.  Don't believe 
that?  Take a look at sales since June.  Can you or anyone justify this 
action?

It wouldn't be a good idea to announce a port to Power4 coinciding with 
the IA-64 port, not with Intel's money financing the port.  :-)

I can suspect that something like that may be happening, with or without 
the knowledge of management.  I wouldn't expect confirmation if it was 
happening.

But, if you can, can you give 'good' reasons that an intellegent person 
can accept, for killing Alpha before IA-64 proves itself capable?

