Alpha is still in the lead.  Alternatively, add 100 to each and you find
the Alpha's percentage advantage diminishes.  Both methods show
diminishing differentiation but Alpha retains the lead.  At the very
least the document could have provided some decent numbers to justify
its conclusion.


3.  The document tries to say that IPF will be cheaper than Alpha at
some date several years away.   (If Compaq can indulge in such
crystal-ball gazing them why couldn't they foresee a collapse of the PC
market a few years ago ?)  The issue is that Compaq are assuming that
the cost of the processor is significant when it is just a part of the
total cost of the system.  Customers are willing to pay it now so why
does Compaq consider that this will become an important isue in future ?


4.  At a number of places, before degrading into sales fluff, the
document shows that the evaluation team wasn't entirely familiar with
what their own company was doing.  For example, they concluded that they
realized the effort to innovate was being put into other areas.  This
realization is a conclusion ?  Give us a break !  At one point they
state that Tru64 and VMS would be enhanced by "a broad server family" -
the meaning of which is not explained - but on page 10 they state that
Compaq is already doing this.
(I could give you several more examples but I think you get the
picture.)


Compaq seem to think that there is no difference between serious
computing at the high-end, where often the software application is the
vital factor, and at the low-end where a computer is used as adjunct to
other work.  They do not understand, or have chosen to ignore, the fact
that an installation like the Pittsburg super computer does not use
packaged software that one buys in a glossy box from the local shop.

Compaq's attempt to justify Alpha's demise appears to be written for the
low-end of the computer market, the market where computers are
commodities, users' skills are limited to mouse-clicking and the market
is more gullible.  This document lacks substance, lacks evidence and
presents conclusions that can't be called conclusions, all merging to
erode any vestige of credibility.  I read it and found that it presented
nothing which would cause me to reconsider my opinion of Compaq's
transfer of Alpha.

I continue to see the demise of Alpha primarily as opportunistic in
directions other than those presented by Compaq.  My suspicion is that
the study into alternative microprocessors was hijacked by those who
want Compaq's financial state to look better than it is (by reducing
expenditure, which happens to be in the only part of the company making
real money) and/or as a way to smarten Compaq up before the wedding with
HP (because HP is Intel-oriented and the merged company would not be
interested in supporting Alpha only for VMS following the demise of
Tru64).



John McLean


