Perhaps it's your view of Alpha's market that is skewed rather than Alpha's
direction.  Leaving aside the fact that EV8 provided noticeable
single-stream performance improvement via its expansion to 8-wide issue from
EV7's 4-wide issue (plus IIRC a couple of other tweaks applicable to
single-stream performance that I'd have to dredge up EV8 documents, if
they're still available, to describe), I suspect that if you analyze Alpha's
(pre-June-25th) market you'll find that most of its revenue came from server
or other applications eminently suited to capitalizing on SMT.

Even workstation and scientific code often can benefit from more than one
execution stream.  EV8 had 4, at a reported cost of about 10% of chip area
over a single-stream implementation, so any installation using as few as two
streams would benefit from it compared with using two separate processors
(with their significantly-increased synchronization latency compared with
on-die multi-streaming) or even approaches that replicate the entire
processor core on-chip (at considerably higher area cost than SMT).

Your viewpoint is hard to distinguish from one that suggests that the ES and
